        "feedback": {
          "reason": "A window owns state for the complete contiguous candidate, not just its endpoints.",
          "details": "Sliding window gains efficiency by updating a summary of the current range when one element enters or leaves. The summary may be a sum, count, frequency map, deque, or another structure, but its semantics cover every member of [left, right]. Pair-oriented two pointers may use only endpoint values or role-specific state such as read and write positions. Both can be linear and use two indices, so those surface properties are not decisive. The state ownership test transfers across implementations even when data structures differ.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "endpoint_values": "Endpoint-only state is consistent with pair-oriented two pointers, not evidence that the whole range is maintained.",
            "two_indices": "Both families and many unrelated algorithms can use two indices; the count does not reveal whether they select a pair, define roles, or bound range-owned state.",
            "linear_loop": "Both pair scans and windows can be amortized linear; runtime shape does not reveal whether the candidate is an endpoint relation or a maintained contiguous range."
          }
        },
