          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Nonnegativity gives the monotone effect required for shrink to restore the budget predicate.",
          "details": "When the current sum exceeds B, extending the same start farther right cannot repair the violation because all future additions are nonnegative. Removing the oldest nonnegative member cannot make the sum larger, so repeated shrinkage eventually restores validity or empties the window. Once a start is removed under this argument, it need not be reconsidered for future right endpoints. The pointer count, optimization objective, and desired complexity do not provide this correctness proof.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "two_indices": "Two moving indices do not establish how removal changes the validity measure.",
            "longest_objective": "A longest-range objective can involve nonmonotone constraints; the objective alone does not prove that removing the leftmost member moves the predicate toward validity.",
            "linear_target": "An O(n) target is a performance requirement, not a correctness argument; it cannot show that discarded starts will never be needed."
          }
        },
