      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-dlc-001",
        "prompt": "You are building a maximum-cardinality schedule from half-open intervals [start, end). Which statement defines the local greedy rule completely?",
        "constraints": [
          "Touching endpoints are compatible.",
          "currentEnd is the end of the last selected interval."
        ],
        "difficulty": "foundational",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "min_end_among_feasible",
              "text": "Among intervals with start >= currentEnd, choose one with minimum end, append it, and set currentEnd to that end."
            },
            {
              "id": "globally_shortest_interval",
              "text": "Choose the globally shortest remaining interval and delete every interval that overlaps it."
            },
            {
              "id": "earliest_start_without_filter",
              "text": "Choose the interval with minimum start, regardless of whether it overlaps the current schedule."
            },
            {
              "id": "latest_start_feasible",
              "text": "Among feasible intervals, choose the one with the latest start because it commits as late as possible."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "min_end_among_feasible"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "A usable local rule must identify the feasible frontier, the direction of comparison, and the state update. Here those are start >= currentEnd, minimum end, and currentEnd = selected.end.",
          "details": "“Pick the earliest-finishing interval” is incomplete unless the current feasibility boundary is part of the rule. After an interval has been selected, only intervals starting at or after currentEnd are candidates. Minimizing end within that set releases the resource as early as possible, and assigning that end to currentEnd defines the residual instance for the next decision.\n\nThe distractors each omit or optimize the wrong component. Short duration does not necessarily produce an early end; earliest start can select an overlapping or very long interval; latest start does not control how long the resource remains occupied. When transferring a greedy rule, write it as a state transition: candidate predicate, comparison key and direction, tie contract if material, selected action, and updated boundary. That formulation can then be tested and proved rather than treated as an intuitive slogan.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "globally_shortest_interval": "Duration appears economical, but a short interval may finish later than another feasible interval and block more future choices. It also ignores the current feasibility frontier.",
            "earliest_start_without_filter": "This choice can overlap the current schedule and therefore is not even a legal candidate. Earliest start also does not minimize the future boundary.",
            "latest_start_feasible": "A late start can still have a very late end. The residual candidate set is controlled by end time, not by how late the selected interval begins."
          }
        },
