      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-dlc-002",
        "prompt": "The interval constraints stay the same, but the objective changes from maximizing the number of selected intervals to maximizing their total weight. What should happen to the local-rule specification?",
        "constraints": [
          "Weights are arbitrary positive numbers.",
          "Intervals remain half-open and pairwise non-overlap is required."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "rule_must_be_rejustified",
              "text": "The earliest-finish rule cannot be reused as a complete rule without a new proof, because a one-for-one exchange may preserve count but lose weight."
            },
            {
              "id": "same_rule_new_label",
              "text": "Keep earliest finish; changing the objective label does not affect the greedy choice."
            },
            {
              "id": "max_weight_each_step",
              "text": "Choose the currently highest-weight feasible interval; the largest local contribution must maximize the total."
            },
            {
              "id": "best_weight_duration_ratio",
              "text": "Choose the greatest weight-to-duration ratio; ratios always convert weighted selection into a greedy problem."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "rule_must_be_rejustified"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "A local rule is defined relative to an objective. Earliest finish preserves future room and cardinality, but it does not preserve arbitrary weight when one interval is exchanged for another.",
          "details": "The unweighted proof exchanges one selected interval for another and keeps the number of selected intervals unchanged. Once intervals have different weights, the same exchange may reduce the objective even when it improves the future boundary. Therefore the old key is not a valid complete rule for the new problem unless an additional property of the weights restores dominance.\n\nChoosing the largest weight or the best ratio is also only a hypothesis. Either choice can block a combination of smaller intervals with greater total weight. The usual weighted-interval formulation retains alternatives by state, but constructing that solution is outside this item. The diagnostic transfer rule is simple: when the objective changes, identify exactly what an exchange or dominance step must preserve. If the old local comparison preserves only a different quantity, the rule must be replaced or re-proved.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "same_rule_new_label": "The objective is part of the correctness contract. Preserving the number of intervals is insufficient when the score is their total weight.",
            "max_weight_each_step": "A single heavy interval may overlap several moderately weighted intervals whose combined value is larger. Immediate contribution does not establish dominance.",
            "best_weight_duration_ratio": "Ratio greedy is valid for some divisible-resource problems, not for arbitrary indivisible interval conflicts. Overlap structure can defeat the ratio order."
          }
