      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-dlc-003",
        "prompt": "At the current boundary, two feasible unweighted intervals have the same minimum end time but different start times. What is the correct tie rule for maximum-cardinality interval selection?",
        "constraints": [
          "Both intervals are feasible against the already selected schedule.",
          "The objective counts intervals and assigns no secondary preference."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "either_same_end",
              "text": "Either tied interval may be selected because both contribute one and create the same next currentEnd."
            },
            {
              "id": "prefer_shorter",
              "text": "The shorter tied interval must be selected because duration is always the secondary greedy key."
            },
            {
              "id": "prefer_earlier_start",
              "text": "The earlier-starting tied interval must be selected to preserve chronological order."
            },
            {
              "id": "tie_invalidates_greedy",
              "text": "Any tie means the local rule is ambiguous and greedy cannot be correct."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "either_same_end"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Under the stated objective, equal-end feasible intervals are equivalent: they add the same count and expose the same future boundary. No extra key is needed unless the product contract adds another objective.",
          "details": "The decisive state for future compatibility is currentEnd. If two feasible choices have the same end, selecting either one produces the same residual candidate set: every later interval must start at or after that shared end. Both also contribute exactly one to the cardinality objective. Their different start times therefore do not affect the proof under the stated assumptions.\n\nA tie rule is not decorative. It must be explicit when tied choices can change provenance, secondary optimization, stability, or future feasibility. For example, if the objective also minimized idle time, preserved original order, or used weights, the start time or another attribute could become material. The transfer boundary is equivalence: arbitrary tie-breaking is safe only after showing that all tied choices have identical consequences for every part of the accepted-answer contract.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "prefer_shorter": "Shorter duration does not change the next boundary when end times are already equal. It would be a new secondary objective not stated in the problem.",
            "prefer_earlier_start": "Chronological order is already respected by feasibility. Earlier start provides no additional future room when the end is tied.",
            "tie_invalidates_greedy": "Ties are harmless when tied choices are equivalent under the invariant and objective. They require a contract, not automatic rejection of the strategy."
          }
        },
