      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-dlc-004",
        "prompt": "Jobs are processed in nondecreasing deadline order. After tentatively adding the next job, total selected duration exceeds the current deadline. Which local rule is fully specified for maximizing the number of completed jobs?",
        "constraints": [
          "Every retained job contributes one to the objective.",
          "The selected jobs are represented by a max-heap keyed by duration."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "remove_longest_selected",
              "text": "Remove the selected job with maximum duration, update total duration, and keep the remaining prefix set."
            },
            {
              "id": "remove_new_job",
              "text": "Always remove the job that was just added because it caused the violation."
            },
            {
              "id": "remove_smallest_selected",
              "text": "Remove the shortest selected job so the remaining jobs are individually substantial."
            },
            {
              "id": "remove_latest_deadline",
              "text": "Remove the selected job with the latest deadline, even though the current prefix is already deadline-ordered."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "remove_longest_selected"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The local repair chooses among currently selected jobs and removes the longest, which frees the most time for the same one-job loss. The heap represents that candidate set efficiently.",
          "details": "The local state is the set of jobs tentatively retained from the current deadline prefix plus their total duration. Once the prefix becomes infeasible, keeping the same cardinality is impossible, so the rule must remove exactly one job. Removing the maximum duration gives the remaining set the minimum possible total duration among all one-job removals from that state. This is the strongest residual state for accommodating later jobs.\n\nThe newly added job is not automatically the worst; it may be short and valuable to keep for cardinality. Removing the shortest releases the least time. Deadline order determines when a prefix constraint is checked, while duration determines the repair choice. The transfer boundary is equal value and prefix feasibility. With unequal rewards or dependencies, “remove longest” may discard too much objective value and requires a different optimization contract.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "remove_new_job": "The new job may be shorter than an earlier retained job. Discarding it can leave a larger total duration than another one-job removal.",
            "remove_smallest_selected": "This frees the least time and therefore produces the weakest remaining prefix for future deadlines.",
            "remove_latest_deadline": "All considered jobs belong to the active prefix. Deadline order exposes the constraint; it does not identify which selected job consumes the most scarce time."
          }
        },
