        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The instance directly disproves the largest-first choice: committing to 4 removes every two-coin optimum. A larger immediate reduction is not the same as a smaller final coin count.",
          "details": "The rule’s first choice is 4, leaving remainder 2, which requires two 1-coins. The resulting count is three. Choosing 3 first leaves another 3 and completes the target with two coins. Therefore no optimal solution for this instance begins with the greedy choice 4. The greedy-choice property fails before any running-time question matters.\n\nSorting only makes the faulty rule deterministic; it does not change the counterexample. Some denomination systems do support largest-first coin change, but that fact must be proved from their denomination structure rather than inferred from sorted values. The transfer habit is to test a local choice against combinations of slightly smaller choices that can jointly outperform it. A single valid counterexample is sufficient to reject a universal greedy rule.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "largest_is_always_safe": "Largest immediate reduction ignores how the remainder can be composed. Here it creates a remainder that needs two additional coins.",
            "sorting_fix": "The algorithm already behaves as descending order would dictate. Ordering cannot repair an invalid dominance claim.",
            "greedy_equals_dp": "Dynamic programming would compare alternative remainders; greedy discards them. Their different behavior is exposed by this counterexample."
