        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Pass choices change which future travel days remain uncovered. The 7-day pass can dominate several locally cheaper purchases, so current price alone is not a sufficient greedy boundary.",
          "details": "Buying eight 1-day passes costs 16. Buying one 7-day pass at day 1 covers days 1 through 7, then one 1-day pass covers day 10, for total cost 9. The locally cheapest action is globally worse because each pass changes the residual position by a different amount. Multiple continuations—pay less now and revisit soon, or pay more and skip several days—must be compared.\n\nSorting pass prices cannot remove this horizon effect, and interval-selection proofs optimize a different output: choosing a compatible subset rather than covering required dates at minimum cost. The transfer signal is variable future coverage or duration. When actions advance the state by different distances and their costs are not dominated, a state-based optimization is a more credible candidate than repeated immediate minimum.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "cheapest_is_always_best": "It minimizes one transaction but can maximize the number of transactions. The objective is the total across the covered horizon.",
