      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-gfs-005",
        "prompt": "Which findings are sufficient reasons to stop treating a proposed greedy rule as established and require a different proof or strategy? Select all statements that apply.",
        "constraints": [
          "The proposal claims correctness for every legal input.",
          "Efficiency is not under evaluation in this item."
        ],
        "difficulty": "advanced",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "multiple",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "valid_counterexample",
              "text": "A legal input on which the rule returns a strictly worse objective value than another feasible solution."
            },
            {
              "id": "lossy_exchange",
              "text": "Replacing an optimal choice with the greedy choice can preserve feasibility but reduce the objective."
            },
            {
              "id": "history_dependent_residual",
              "text": "Two partial solutions at the same visible boundary have different future possibilities because discarded history still matters."
            },
            {
              "id": "requires_sorting",
              "text": "The method begins with sorting, which by itself shows that the local choices are unsafe."
            },
            {
              "id": "uses_heap",
              "text": "The method maintains a heap, which by itself shows that the local choices are unsafe."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "valid_counterexample",
            "lossy_exchange",
            "history_dependent_residual"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Each accepted finding breaks a correctness obligation: universal validity, objective-preserving exchange, or sufficient residual state. Sorting and heaps are neutral implementation mechanisms.",
          "details": "A universal greedy rule is immediately refuted by one valid counterexample. Even without a full counterexample, a proposed exchange that loses objective value cannot establish compatibility with an optimum. A residual state that omits history also blocks repeated local commitment because later outcomes depend on alternatives the rule discarded.\n\nThese diagnostics are stronger than implementation observations. Sorting may expose a valid order, and a heap may maintain a proven dynamic extreme; neither is evidence for or against correctness by itself. The transfer checklist is therefore strategy-neutral: test the rule on adversarial combinations, inspect every exchange for both feasibility and objective preservation, and verify that the updated state captures all future consequences. A failed test does not automatically prove which replacement strategy is correct, but it does prevent unsupported activation of the greedy claim.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "requires_sorting": "Many correct greedy algorithms sort. Sorting becomes a problem only when the chosen key lacks a correctness relation.",
            "uses_heap": "A heap is an implementation tool for dynamic extremes and appears in correct greedy algorithms. Its presence is not a failure signal."
          },
          "omittedCorrectExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "valid_counterexample": "A single legal counterexample disproves a rule claimed for all inputs, regardless of how many examples passed.",
            "lossy_exchange": "If the exchange lowers the objective, it cannot transform an optimum into a greedy-compatible optimum.",
            "history_dependent_residual": "Hidden relevant history means the greedy boundary is insufficient and later local choices may be based on incomplete state."
          }
        },
