      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-gmr-001",
        "prompt": "A solution note says: “The algorithm sorts once, scans once, and matches all twelve examples, so the greedy choice is correct.” What is the primary defect?",
        "constraints": [
          "The note claims correctness for every legal input.",
          "The examples are not an exhaustive finite domain."
        ],
        "difficulty": "foundational",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "missing_universal_safety",
              "text": "The note gives efficiency and observations but no argument that the local choice preserves an optimum on every input."
            },
            {
              "id": "sorting_too_slow",
              "text": "Sorting is too slow for any greedy algorithm."
            },
            {
              "id": "needs_more_examples_only",
              "text": "The proof becomes complete after adding a sufficiently large informal example set."
            },
            {
              "id": "greedy_cannot_sort",
              "text": "A method that sorts is not greedy, so the strategy label is wrong."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "missing_universal_safety"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Complexity and examples answer different questions from correctness. The missing link is a universal safety argument for the irreversible local choice.",
          "details": "O(n log n) may be an acceptable cost, and examples may increase confidence or reveal bugs, but neither explains why the algorithm cannot make an irreversible mistake on an untested instance. A greedy proof must connect the chosen local preference to a global optimum, such as by exchange, dominance, a stays-ahead invariant, or induction after a safe first choice.\n\nMore non-exhaustive examples never create that quantification. Sorting is compatible with many greedy methods and is not the defect. The review habit is to separate claims: implementation complexity, empirical behavior, feasibility, and optimality each require their own evidence. Tests are powerful for finding counterexamples; they are not a substitute for the correctness contract.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "sorting_too_slow": "Sorting can be fully acceptable and often defines the greedy processing order. Speed is not the missing correctness argument.",
            "needs_more_examples_only": "Unless the legal domain is exhaustively finite and fully enumerated, additional examples still do not prove the universal claim.",
            "greedy_cannot_sort": "Sort-then-greedy is a standard strategy shape. Greedy refers to the commitment rule, not the absence of preprocessing."
          }
        },
