      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-gmr-002",
        "prompt": "A reviewer writes: “Sort projects by descending reward. The highest reward is obviously the best first choice, so no further justification is needed.” Which diagnosis is most precise?",
        "constraints": [
          "Projects can conflict with several other projects.",
          "The objective is total reward of the selected compatible set."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "key_ignores_opportunity_cost",
              "text": "The claim compares individual rewards but does not show that the first project can replace the compatible set it excludes without losing total value."
            },
            {
              "id": "descending_never_allowed",
              "text": "Descending sort direction is invalid in every greedy algorithm."
            },
            {
              "id": "reward_not_numeric",
              "text": "Reward cannot be used as a numeric key."
            },
            {
              "id": "proof_by_adjective",
              "text": "Calling the choice obvious is a formal proof because the objective also mentions reward."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "key_ignores_opportunity_cost"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The argument ignores opportunity cost and gives no exchange relation. A highest individual reward can block several compatible rewards whose total is larger.",
          "details": "A sort key must order candidates by a property that controls the residual problem or objective. Descending reward compares each project in isolation. If the first project conflicts with several others, its true consequence includes the value of every combination it removes. The note never proves that replacing those projects with the highest-reward project is no worse.\n\nDescending order is not inherently invalid, and reward can be a legitimate numeric field in problems with the right exchange structure. The defect is the missing structural link, not the data type or direction. The transfer rule is to challenge words such as “obvious,” “natural,” or “largest” by asking what residual boundary the key improves and what optimal exchange it permits.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "descending_never_allowed": "Some correct greedy algorithms maximize a key. Direction must be tied to the proof, not banned categorically.",
            "reward_not_numeric": "Reward is numeric here. The problem is not representability but failure to account for interactions among selected projects.",
            "proof_by_adjective": "Restating the objective with an adjective does not show that the local winner is compatible with a global optimum."
          }
        },
