      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-gmr-003",
        "prompt": "A design document says: “The problem has optimal substructure, so greedy and dynamic programming are interchangeable; choose whichever is easier to code.” What is the correct review comment?",
        "constraints": [
          "The document has not established a greedy-choice property.",
          "Several partial states may lead to different future values."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "methods_need_different_property",
              "text": "Optimal substructure alone does not justify greedy; greedy also needs a safe local commitment, while dynamic programming may compare multiple states."
            },
            {
              "id": "greedy_always_faster",
              "text": "Greedy is interchangeable with dynamic programming whenever it has lower running time."
            },
            {
              "id": "dp_is_not_optimization",
              "text": "Dynamic programming cannot solve optimization problems."
            },
            {
              "id": "same_recursion",
              "text": "Any recursive optimal-substructure proof is automatically a greedy proof."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "methods_need_different_property"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The methods share neither state retention nor proof obligations. Optimal substructure supports decomposition, but greedy additionally needs a theorem that discarding alternatives is safe.",
          "details": "Dynamic programming can exploit optimal substructure while retaining several states that encode different histories or resource levels. Greedy commits to one local choice and removes competing possibilities. That stronger discard requires a greedy-choice property, exchange argument, dominance relation, or stays-ahead invariant. Without it, the fact that an optimal solution contains optimal subsolutions does not identify which first choice is safe.\n\nRunning time cannot make two incorrect and correct methods interchangeable, and both methods can solve optimization problems. Recursion is an implementation or proof form, not a strategy classification. The transfer rule is to ask what alternatives remain alive after each step. If multiple materially different states must be compared, dynamic programming may fit; if one state dominates all others by proof, greedy may fit.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "greedy_always_faster": "Efficiency cannot supply a missing correctness property. A faster wrong algorithm is not interchangeable with a correct state-based one.",
            "dp_is_not_optimization": "Optimization is a primary use of dynamic programming. This option reverses the method’s purpose.",
            "same_recursion": "A recursive decomposition can still compare multiple choices. Greedy correctness depends on safe commitment, not on whether recursion is present."
          }
        },
