      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-gmr-005",
        "prompt": "An author claims: “Sort intervals by earliest start and keep each non-overlapping interval. This is optimal because it starts work as soon as possible; several examples pass. Ties and touching endpoints need no definition.” Which review findings are valid? Select all statements that apply.",
        "constraints": [
          "The objective is maximum selected count.",
          "The interval endpoint model has not been specified elsewhere."
        ],
        "difficulty": "advanced",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "multiple",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "start_not_future_boundary",
              "text": "Earliest start does not control when the resource becomes free; a long early interval can block a larger compatible set."
            },
            {
              "id": "examples_not_exchange",
              "text": "Passing examples do not establish that an optimum can exchange its first interval for the proposed choice."
            },
            {
              "id": "endpoint_contract_missing",
              "text": "Whether touching intervals are compatible changes the acceptance inequality and must be stated."
            },
            {
              "id": "sorting_requires_dp",
              "text": "Any algorithm that sorts must be replaced by dynamic programming."
            },
            {
              "id": "greedy_never_optimal",
              "text": "No interval problem can have a correct greedy solution."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "start_not_future_boundary",
            "examples_not_exchange",
            "endpoint_contract_missing"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The proposal has three independent defects: the key optimizes the wrong boundary, examples do not prove safety, and compatibility is ambiguous without endpoint semantics.",
          "details": "Maximum-cardinality interval selection is governed by the end of the selected interval because that end determines which future intervals remain feasible. Earliest start can select a long interval that blocks several later intervals. Even if examples happen to avoid that case, they do not show that every optimum can be transformed to begin with the earliest-start choice. Finally, “non-overlapping” is incomplete until touching endpoints are classified, because the scan must choose between >= and > at the boundary.\n\nSorting itself is compatible with a correct earliest-finish greedy algorithm, and interval problems include both greedy-solvable and non-greedy variants. The review transfer template is: verify the local key controls the decisive residual state, require universal safety evidence, and surface all assumptions that alter feasibility before the learner answers.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "sorting_requires_dp": "Sorting is a preprocessing step used by many correct greedy algorithms. The defect is the unsupported key, not sorting itself.",
            "greedy_never_optimal": "Unweighted maximum-cardinality interval selection has a correct earliest-finish greedy solution. The family cannot be rejected categorically."
          },
          "omittedCorrectExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "start_not_future_boundary": "Without this finding, the review misses the central strategy error: start time is not the residual compatibility boundary.",
            "examples_not_exchange": "Without this finding, empirical agreement is incorrectly treated as a universal optimality proof.",
            "endpoint_contract_missing": "Without this finding, the implementation and accepted schedule remain ambiguous for intervals that touch."
          }
        },
