      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-ig-001",
        "prompt": "A schedule uses half-open intervals [start, end), and currentEnd is the end of the last selected interval. Which condition makes a candidate interval compatible?",
        "constraints": [
          "The resource is released at end and may be reused immediately.",
          "Zero-length intervals are outside this item’s scope."
        ],
        "difficulty": "foundational",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "start_gte_current_end",
              "text": "The candidate start is greater than or equal to currentEnd."
            },
            {
              "id": "start_gt_current_end",
              "text": "The candidate start is strictly greater than currentEnd."
            },
            {
              "id": "end_gte_current_end",
              "text": "The candidate end is greater than or equal to currentEnd."
            },
            {
              "id": "duration_lt_gap",
              "text": "candidate duration < currentEnd - candidate.start"
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "start_gte_current_end"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Half-open intervals exclude their end point, so a candidate beginning exactly at currentEnd does not overlap the selected interval. The acceptance boundary is start >= currentEnd.",
          "details": "The endpoint model is part of the algorithm, not a formatting detail. A selected interval [a,currentEnd) occupies times t with a <= t < currentEnd. At t = currentEnd the resource is free, so [currentEnd,b) is compatible. Requiring a strict greater-than would incorrectly reject legal back-to-back intervals and could reduce the maximum count.\n\nChecking candidate.end against currentEnd does not establish non-overlap because an interval can end later while starting inside the occupied range. The transfer rule is to derive the inequality from the domain contract. Closed intervals, discrete inclusive ranges, cleanup gaps, or travel time between meetings can require start > currentEnd or start >= currentEnd + gap. Hidden endpoint assumptions must not appear only in feedback.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "start_gt_current_end": "This introduces an unstated positive gap and rejects intervals that begin exactly when the previous half-open interval releases the resource.",
            "end_gte_current_end": "A later end does not prevent overlap. Compatibility depends on the candidate start relative to the occupied boundary.",
            "duration_lt_gap": "The expression does not represent interval overlap and can be negative or unrelated to the candidate’s start position."
          }
        },
