        "feedback": {
          "reason": "X is infeasible because it starts before 5. Y and Z are feasible, and Y finishes earlier, so it creates the strongest next boundary.",
          "details": "The scan performs two logically separate operations. First it filters candidates by compatibility: X starts at 4 and overlaps the selected interval, while Y starts exactly at 5 and is legal under half-open semantics; Z is also legal. Second it applies the greedy comparison among feasible candidates. Y ends at 7, earlier than Z at 9, so selecting Y leaves at least as much room for later intervals.\n\nChoosing Z would still be feasible but would abandon the earliest-finish dominance that supports maximum cardinality. Selecting none would misuse endpoint semantics. This boundary application transfers to meetings, machine reservations, or event selection when the resource is reusable immediately and every accepted interval has equal value. Setup gaps or weights change the filter or objective.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "select_x": "X overlaps the occupied range because its start is before currentEnd. Its early finish does not make an infeasible choice legal.",
            "select_z": "Z is feasible, but it finishes later than Y and therefore leaves a weaker residual boundary for no objective benefit.",
            "select_none": "Y begins exactly at the half-open release point and is compatible. Touching is not overlap under the stated contract."
