      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-ig-004",
        "prompt": "Intervals are still pairwise incompatible when they overlap, but each interval now has an arbitrary positive weight and the objective is maximum total weight. What is the correct boundary conclusion?",
        "constraints": [
          "Weights are independent of start and end times.",
          "The problem remains offline and all intervals are known."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "earliest_finish_not_sufficient",
              "text": "Earliest finish is no longer sufficient because its exchange can preserve feasibility and count while decreasing total weight."
            },
            {
              "id": "earliest_finish_still_exact",
              "text": "Earliest finish remains optimal because future room is the only property that matters."
            },
            {
              "id": "highest_weight_greedy",
              "text": "Choosing the highest-weight feasible interval is always optimal."
            },
            {
              "id": "methods_interchangeable",
              "text": "Greedy and dynamic programming are interchangeable, so either proof applies unchanged."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "earliest_finish_not_sufficient"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Weights add an objective dimension that the end boundary does not dominate. The unweighted exchange preserves one selected item, but it may replace a valuable interval with a low-value one.",
          "details": "Earliest finish optimizes future capacity, not current weight. In the unweighted problem, exchanging one interval for another preserves the one-unit contribution. With arbitrary weights, the earlier-finishing interval may be worth far less, and the extra future room may not compensate. Conversely, choosing the heaviest interval can block a combination with greater total weight.\n\nThe usual diagnostic is to retain alternatives at a boundary such as the next compatible interval and compare include versus exclude value. That suggests dynamic programming, but this item stops at the strategy boundary rather than defining the recurrence. Greedy and dynamic programming are not interchangeable: greedy needs a local dominance theorem; dynamic programming can compare state-dependent continuations when that theorem is absent.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "earliest_finish_still_exact": "Future room is only one part of the weighted objective. A feasible exchange can lose more weight than the extra room can recover.",
            "highest_weight_greedy": "The heaviest interval may overlap several moderately weighted intervals whose combined value is larger.",
            "methods_interchangeable": "The proof obligations differ. The failure of a greedy-choice property is precisely why a state-based comparison may be needed."
          }
        },
