      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-psc-001",
        "prompt": "Let g be the feasible interval with the earliest finish, and let an optimal maximum-cardinality schedule begin with interval o. Which statement proves that choosing g first is safe?",
        "constraints": [
          "Intervals are half-open and unweighted.",
          "g.end <= o.end by definition of g."
        ],
        "difficulty": "foundational",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "replace_o_with_g",
              "text": "Replace o with g; every interval that followed o still starts after g ends, so feasibility and selected count are preserved."
            },
            {
              "id": "g_is_shortest",
              "text": "g must be the shortest interval, so it consumes the least resource time."
            },
            {
              "id": "g_appears_first_sorted",
              "text": "g appears first after sorting, and the first sorted element is always safe."
            },
            {
              "id": "examples_show_safety",
              "text": "A few schedules start with g and reach the optimum count, so the choice is proven for all inputs."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "replace_o_with_g"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Safety follows from a no-worse exchange: g ends no later than the optimal first interval, so replacing that interval with g preserves every later choice and the schedule size.",
          "details": "The proof must connect the local choice to an arbitrary optimum. Start with an optimal schedule O whose first interval is o. Since g has the earliest feasible end, g.end <= o.end. Every later interval in O starts at or after o.end; therefore it also starts at or after g.end. Replacing o with g yields another feasible schedule with exactly the same number of intervals. Consequently at least one optimum begins with g, so committing to g does not discard all optimal solutions.\n\nThis is the greedy-choice property. It is not derived from g being short, from its position in a sorted array, or from observed examples. The transfer rule is to identify what the replacement improves or preserves: the residual feasibility boundary must be no worse, and the exchanged choice must contribute the same amount to the objective. If either condition changes, the proof must change.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "g_is_shortest": "Earliest finish does not imply shortest duration, and duration is not the boundary used by later compatibility. A short interval can still finish late.",
            "g_appears_first_sorted": "Sorting defines processing order but does not prove that selecting the first item preserves an optimum. The exchange relation supplies that proof.",
            "examples_show_safety": "Examples cannot quantify over every optimal schedule. A proof must show why any optimum can be transformed without loss."
          }
        },
