      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-psc-002",
        "prompt": "A deadline-ordered prefix becomes infeasible after a job is added. The candidate algorithm removes the selected job with greatest duration. Which argument makes that removal safe for maximizing completed-job count?",
        "constraints": [
          "Exactly one selected job must be removed to reduce the prefix count by one.",
          "All retained jobs contribute one unit to the objective."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "minimum_remaining_duration",
              "text": "For the same retained count, removing the longest gives the smallest possible remaining total duration among all one-job removals, leaving the strongest prefix for future deadlines."
            },
            {
              "id": "long_jobs_are_bad",
              "text": "Long jobs are generally undesirable, so deleting one is intuitively optimal."
            },
            {
              "id": "heap_top_is_proof",
              "text": "The job is at the top of the max-heap, which makes it the mathematically correct deletion."
            },
            {
              "id": "new_job_caused_failure",
              "text": "The new job caused the overflow, so removing the longest is safe only when the new job is also the longest."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "minimum_remaining_duration"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The proof compares all one-job repairs of the same prefix. Removing the longest minimizes the retained total duration without changing retained count, so no alternative repair leaves more time for later jobs.",
          "details": "When the prefix is infeasible, retaining all selected jobs is impossible under the current deadline. Any repair that keeps the maximum possible cardinality at this moment removes one job. Among those repairs, deleting the maximum duration minimizes the total time of the remaining jobs. This remaining state dominates every other one-job repair: it has the same count and no larger time consumption. Later deadline checks therefore start from at least as much free time.\n\nThe proof depends on the objective counting jobs equally. If jobs have unequal reward, the longest job may also carry most of the value, and minimum retained duration no longer dominates total reward. It also depends on deadline prefixes being the relevant constraints. The heap merely returns the longest duration efficiently; the safety argument is the comparison between residual states.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "long_jobs_are_bad": "Length is not globally bad by itself. The precise fact is comparative: within the same selected prefix and one-job removal, deleting the longest minimizes remaining time.",
            "heap_top_is_proof": "A data structure can implement an extreme query but cannot establish that the queried extreme preserves the objective or invariant.",
            "new_job_caused_failure": "The new job need not be the best job to remove. An earlier, longer job may be replaced by the new shorter job to improve the residual prefix."
          }
        },
        "taxonomy": {
