      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-psc-003",
        "prompt": "In an exchange proof, replacing an optimal interval o with an earlier-finishing interval g keeps every later interval feasible. What additional fact is still required before the exchange proves safety?",
        "constraints": [
          "The prompt does not state whether intervals are weighted.",
          "The replacement changes only the first selected interval."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "preserve_objective_value",
              "text": "The exchange must also show that replacing o with g does not reduce the objective value under the actual scoring contract."
            },
            {
              "id": "g_has_earlier_start",
              "text": "g must start earlier than o, even though later feasibility depends on end time."
            },
            {
              "id": "sorting_is_stable",
              "text": "The input sort must be stable so equal keys keep their original order."
            },
            {
              "id": "greedy_label_suffices",
              "text": "No additional fact is needed because feasibility preservation alone proves every greedy objective."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "preserve_objective_value"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "A safe exchange preserves both feasibility and the optimized quantity. Earlier finish protects future choices, but arbitrary weights can make the replacement lose value.",
          "details": "Correctness has two independent dimensions. Feasibility asks whether the transformed solution still satisfies all constraints. Objective preservation asks whether its score is at least as good as the original optimum. For unweighted maximum-cardinality intervals, a one-for-one replacement preserves the count automatically. For weighted intervals, g may finish earlier but have much lower weight than o, so the transformed schedule can be feasible and still cease to be optimal.\n\nThis distinction is a useful review tool for every greedy proof. Identify the exact constraint preserved by the local dominance relation, then identify the exact objective quantity preserved or improved. A proof that addresses only one side is incomplete. Stable sorting and start time are irrelevant unless the problem contract makes them material. The greedy label never supplies a missing objective argument.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "g_has_earlier_start": "The future compatibility boundary is the finish time. Earlier start neither follows from earliest finish nor repairs a loss in objective value.",
            "sorting_is_stable": "Stable order can matter for reproducibility, but it does not show that exchanging selected items preserves the optimization score.",
            "greedy_label_suffices": "Feasibility alone is insufficient. A feasible transformed solution can have a lower score than the optimum it replaced."
          }
        },
