      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-psc-004",
        "prompt": "Suppose a proof has shown that some optimal solution begins with the greedy choice g. What statement is needed to justify solving the rest by applying the same greedy rule again?",
        "constraints": [
          "The residual instance contains exactly the choices compatible with g.",
          "The objective is additive over selected choices or counts them equally."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "residual_must_be_optimal",
              "text": "The suffix of that optimal solution must itself be optimal for the residual instance; otherwise replacing the suffix would improve the original solution."
            },
            {
              "id": "same_code_path",
              "text": "The implementation calls the same loop again, so the residual problem is automatically optimal."
            },
            {
              "id": "dp_and_greedy_same",
              "text": "Dynamic programming has optimal substructure, so greedy may use the same proof without a greedy-choice property."
            },
            {
              "id": "future_choices_irrelevant",
              "text": "Once the first choice is safe, any feasible sequence of later choices is globally optimal."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "residual_must_be_optimal"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "A safe first choice is only the first induction step. The remainder of an optimum must be optimal for the residual instance, allowing the same argument to repeat.",
          "details": "Assume an optimal full solution starts with g. Remove g and consider the remaining compatible choices. If the suffix were not optimal for that residual instance, a better residual solution could replace it, producing a full solution better than the supposed optimum. That contradiction establishes optimal substructure for the restricted problem. The greedy-choice proof can then be applied inductively at the next boundary.\n\nThis does not make greedy and dynamic programming interchangeable. Both may use optimal substructure, but greedy additionally discards alternatives after proving one local choice safe. Dynamic programming may retain several states when no such choice property exists. The transfer condition is state sufficiency: the updated boundary must capture every past consequence relevant to future feasibility and value. If hidden history remains, repeatedly applying the same local rule is not justified.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "same_code_path": "Reusing code does not prove that the mathematical residual instance has the same objective or that its chosen suffix is optimal.",
            "dp_and_greedy_same": "Optimal substructure alone is not the greedy-choice property. Greedy needs a safe commitment that dynamic programming does not assume.",
            "future_choices_irrelevant": "A safe first choice can still be followed by a suboptimal suffix. The residual choices must be optimized, not chosen arbitrarily."
          }
        },
