        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The stays-ahead invariant compares reach after the same number of stops. Selecting the largest fuel from the reachable frontier cannot leave the vehicle behind another equal-stop strategy.",
          "details": "When another refuel becomes necessary, every station in the heap has already been passed and was reachable before the current failure point. For a fixed number of refuels, replacing any smaller chosen fuel amount with the largest available one cannot reduce total fuel obtained or reachable distance. Therefore the greedy strategy stays at least as far ahead as any alternative using the same number of stops. If greedy needs another stop, no strategy with fewer stops could have reached farther using only the same previously reachable stations.\n\nThe heap implements the frontier maximum, but the invariant proves the stop count. The transfer boundary matters: candidates must already be feasible and their benefit must combine monotonically. If selecting fuel changed which earlier stations had been reachable, or if fuel had penalties coupled across choices, the stays-ahead comparison would need a different state and might fail.",
