      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-stg-002",
        "prompt": "You must select the maximum number of pairwise compatible half-open intervals. Which sort key prepares the canonical greedy scan?",
        "constraints": [
          "Each interval contributes one to the objective.",
          "The scan accepts an interval only when its start is at least currentEnd."
        ],
        "difficulty": "foundational",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "finish_ascending",
              "text": "Sort by nondecreasing finish time."
            },
            {
              "id": "start_ascending",
              "text": "Sort by nondecreasing start time."
            },
            {
              "id": "duration_ascending",
              "text": "Sort by nondecreasing duration."
            },
            {
              "id": "finish_descending",
              "text": "Sort by nonincreasing finish time."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "finish_ascending"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Ascending finish time exposes the feasible choice that releases the resource earliest. That is the boundary used by the exchange proof and the later scan.",
          "details": "The scan maintains currentEnd, the end of the last accepted interval. Among feasible candidates, the interval with the smallest finish produces the smallest next currentEnd and therefore leaves the largest possible set of later intervals eligible. Sorting by finish makes that candidate appear first whenever the scan advances.\n\nStart order can commit to a very long interval; duration order can prefer a short interval that ends late; descending finish order intentionally closes the future frontier. The sort key and the acceptance predicate are separate parts of the algorithm: sorting prepares the order, while the scan still rejects intervals whose start is before currentEnd. The transfer boundary is equal contribution per interval. With weights, preserving an early boundary does not necessarily preserve value.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "start_ascending": "Early start is not the limiting future state. An interval can start first and finish so late that it blocks several compatible choices.",
            "duration_ascending": "Short duration does not imply early finish. A short interval placed late can be worse for the future boundary than a longer interval ending earlier.",
            "finish_descending": "This chooses the interval that releases the resource latest, the opposite of the exchange-safe boundary for maximum count."
          }
        },
