      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-stg-004",
        "prompt": "All jobs are available at time 0 on one machine, and the objective is to minimize the sum of completion times. Which processing-time order is the justified greedy order?",
        "constraints": [
          "Jobs are non-preemptive and have no weights or deadlines.",
          "Completion time includes all processing before and including the job."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "processing_time_ascending",
              "text": "Sort by nondecreasing processing time."
            },
            {
              "id": "processing_time_descending",
              "text": "Sort by nonincreasing processing time."
            },
            {
              "id": "deadline_ascending",
              "text": "Sort by deadline even though no deadlines are present."
            },
            {
              "id": "arbitrary_order",
              "text": "Any order has the same sum because total processing time is fixed."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "processing_time_ascending"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Short jobs first minimizes repeated waiting contribution. Swapping an adjacent long-before-short pair reduces the sum of their completion times without affecting later completion times.",
          "details": "For adjacent jobs with durations a > b beginning at time t, order a,b contributes completion times (t+a) and (t+a+b). Order b,a contributes (t+b) and (t+b+a). The second job in either order finishes at the same time, but the first completion is smaller by a-b when the shorter job is first. Later jobs begin at the same time in both schedules. Repeatedly removing long-before-short inversions yields nondecreasing processing time.\n\nThe fixed total duration does not make sums equal because earlier processing is counted in multiple jobs’ completion times. This key solves a different objective from earliest-deadline or maximum-cardinality scheduling. Weights, release times, precedence, or preemption introduce new decision boundaries and may require another key or method.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "processing_time_descending": "Long jobs first maximize rather than minimize the waiting imposed on many later jobs under this objective.",
            "deadline_ascending": "No deadline field exists in the contract. Earliest-deadline order is tied to maximum lateness, not sum of completion times.",
            "arbitrary_order": "Although the final makespan is fixed, individual completion times and their sum depend on the order."
          }
        },
