      {
        "id": "alg-greedy-intro-stg-005",
        "prompt": "In earliest-deadline order for minimizing maximum lateness, two adjacent jobs have the same deadline but different processing times. What tie rule is required under the stated objective?",
        "constraints": [
          "All jobs are available at time 0 on one non-preemptive machine.",
          "There is no secondary objective such as minimizing total completion time."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "either_equal_deadline_order",
              "text": "Either order is valid for the maximum-lateness optimum because both are consistent with nondecreasing deadline order."
            },
            {
              "id": "shorter_must_win",
              "text": "The shorter job must come first or earliest-deadline order becomes incorrect."
            },
            {
              "id": "longer_must_win",
              "text": "The longer job must come first to prevent it from becoming late."
            },
            {
              "id": "tie_requires_dp",
              "text": "A deadline tie creates two states, so dynamic programming is required."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "either_equal_deadline_order"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Equal deadlines create no deadline inversion. Under the single stated objective, either tied order remains an earliest-deadline schedule and preserves optimal maximum lateness.",
          "details": "The exchange proof removes pairs with d_i > d_j. When deadlines are equal, neither order violates nondecreasing deadline order. The pair completes at the same final time in either order, and the maximum lateness of the pair is determined by that shared deadline and the pair’s completion times. Any order among equal deadlines belongs to the proven optimal family.\n\nA product may still choose a deterministic secondary key, such as stable input order, for reproducibility. That engineering choice must not be presented as required for the mathematical objective. Shorter-first would become material if a secondary sum-completion objective were added; longer-first has no general guarantee. A tie creates alternatives, but alternatives alone do not imply dynamic programming when the proof shows them equivalent.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "shorter_must_win": "Shorter-first may improve a separate objective, but it is not required for optimal maximum lateness when deadlines are equal.",
            "longer_must_win": "Processing the longer job first can increase the first completion time and has no special guarantee under equal deadlines.",
            "tie_requires_dp": "Dynamic programming is needed when alternatives have materially different future consequences. These tied orders are equivalent for the stated proof and objective."
          }
        },
