        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The process has two distinct decisions: deadline order activates constraints, and a max-heap identifies the best local repair when the retained prefix becomes infeasible.",
          "details": "A static sort can determine when candidates are considered without determining which selected candidate remains best to keep. In the deadline-cardinality problem, a newly considered short job may be worth keeping in place of a much longer earlier job. The feasible set and its decisive extreme change after each insertion, so the algorithm maintains selected durations and queries their maximum when repair is needed.\n\nCalling the method greedy depends on irreversible dominance decisions, not on using only one data structure. There is no requirement that one key serve every role. The transfer boundary is whether the ordered scan makes earlier acceptances permanent. When later information can safely replace a retained candidate, sorting may need a heap, stack, or running extreme to implement the proven local update.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
