        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Crossed and single-position intervals have no remaining pair, even though only one of them is empty.",
          "details": "Recursion stops when the current problem has become atomic under the chosen decomposition. A crossed\ninterval has no characters, and a single-position interval has one character but no pair to compare. Both\nare already palindromes under this contract. A two-character interval is different: its one outer pair is\nstill the material decision. This boundary shows why recursive structure is not tied to an empty-input\nkeyword. The terminal shape follows from what the recursive step removes. The same reasoning applies when\npeeling matching delimiters, trimming equal endpoints, or consuming two items per call: both zero-item and\none-item remainders may be atomic.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
