          "details": "Every call to digits has one promise: return the count of decimal digits in its own argument. Values below\nten are terminal because they contain exactly one digit, including zero. For a larger value, integer\ndivision by ten removes one trailing digit and leaves a smaller non-negative integer whose digit count has\nthe same meaning. Adding one reconstructs the current answer. A base that returns the numeric digit itself\nor treats zero as having no digits breaks the uniform return contract. The transfer rule is to state the\nmeaning before choosing a sentinel value; the base return must be a real answer for that terminal state.",
