      {
        "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-mistake-review-binary-tree-two-calls-not-exponential-in-n",
        "prompt": "nodeCount(node) returns 0 for None and otherwise returns\n1 + nodeCount(node.left) + nodeCount(node.right) on a finite binary tree containing n real nodes.\nA review labels it O(2^n) because every frame has two call sites. Which correction is precise?",
        "constraints": [
          "The input is a tree rather than a DAG with shared child nodes.",
          "Per-node local work is O(1).",
          "h denotes tree height in nodes."
        ],
        "difficulty": "advanced",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "linear_by_distinct_nodes",
              "text": "It is O(n) time because each real node is the argument of exactly one non-null call; stack space is O(h)."
            },
            {
              "id": "exponential_call_sites",
              "text": "The O(2^n) label is correct whenever source code contains two recursive calls."
            },
            {
              "id": "log_time_balanced",
              "text": "It is O(log n) time on a balanced tree because height is logarithmic."
            },
            {
              "id": "constant_after_base",
              "text": "It is O(1) because each child eventually returns the constant 0 base."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "linear_by_distinct_nodes"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The recursion follows disjoint child ownership, so total calls scale with actual nodes while simultaneous frames scale with height.",
          "details": "Each non-null call owns one distinct real node and creates calls only for that node's children. In a tree, no child has two parents, so no non-null state is recomputed. There are n non-null calls and O(n) null-child calls, yielding O(n) time. During depth-first execution, at most one root-to-current path is active, giving O(h) stack space. A full tree can have roughly 2^h nodes, but that is still linear in n; expressing cost in height does not justify calling it exponential in the input size n.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "exponential_call_sites": "Call sites do not determine repeated states. The child subtrees are disjoint and the finite input contains only n real nodes to visit.",
            "log_time_balanced": "Balanced height reduces maximum stack depth, but all n nodes still contribute to the count and must be visited.",
            "constant_after_base": "The base is reached through a number of calls proportional to the tree's nodes and null edges; terminal values do not erase traversal work."
          }
        },
