        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "A base predicate protects terminal-state operations only when it is checked before those operations.",
          "details": "The state i == len(a) is a legal empty suffix under the function's contract. Accessing a[i] is invalid there, so the terminal guard must precede the element comparison. Merely having the correct predicate later in the function does not cover the state. This is distinct from non-progress: i advances correctly and would reach the terminal index, but the function violates the domain before it can return. The transfer rule applies whenever terminal states lack resources used by the recursive case, such as a node value, array element, child pointer, or divisor.",
