      {
        "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-mistake-review-shared-global-depth-contamination",
        "prompt": "A tree traversal records each leaf's depth using one global variable depth:\n\n    visit(node):\n        if node is None: return\n        depth += 1\n        if node is a leaf: record(depth)\n        visit(node.left)\n        visit(node.right)\n\nThere is no decrement before a frame returns. What is the defect?",
        "constraints": [
          "The traversal may visit both left and right children.",
          "depth is one mutable global variable.",
          "No restoration occurs on return."
        ],
        "difficulty": "advanced",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "sibling_inherits_prior_branch",
              "text": "The left traversal leaves depth increased, so the right sibling is measured using state from the completed left branch."
            },
            {
              "id": "all_frames_must_share_depth",
              "text": "The code is correct because every recursive call has identical state and therefore should use one global depth."
            },
            {
              "id": "base_should_increment",
              "text": "The None base case should increment depth to balance the traversal."
            },
            {
              "id": "only_stack_overflow",
              "text": "The values are correct; the only risk is stack overflow."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "sibling_inherits_prior_branch"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "A mutable path-dependent value must be frame-local or restored on unwind; otherwise completed branches contaminate later siblings.",
          "details": "Each call conceptually has a current depth derived from its parent. A parameter such as visit(node, depth) naturally gives every frame its own value. A shared mutable alternative can work only with a strict push/pop discipline: increment before children and decrement before returning. The shown code never restores depth, so after the left subtree finishes, the global value still reflects its deepest path when the right subtree begins. The false heuristic is confusing identical recursive schema with identical or globally shared runtime state.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "all_frames_must_share_depth": "Calls share a contract, not one mutable contextual value. Different active paths require distinct depths or disciplined restore behavior.",
            "base_should_increment": "Null children are not real path steps. Incrementing there adds more error and does not restore ancestor state.",
            "only_stack_overflow": "Even on a shallow tree, recorded right-branch depths can be wrong because the global variable is not restored after the left branch."
          }
        },
