      {
        "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-stack-and-complexity-tree-dfs-work-versus-height",
        "prompt": "A depth-first recursive traversal visits each of n real tree nodes once. Let h be the number of real nodes\non the longest root-to-leaf path. Which asymptotic statement is correct?",
        "constraints": [
          "The structure is a tree with no shared or cyclic nodes.",
          "Each real node is visited exactly once.",
          "Per-node local work is O(1)."
        ],
        "difficulty": "introductory",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "time_n_stack_h",
              "text": "Time is O(n), and recursion-stack space is O(h)."
            },
            {
              "id": "time_h_stack_n",
              "text": "Time is O(h), and recursion-stack space is O(n)."
            },
            {
              "id": "time_n_stack_n_always",
              "text": "Time is O(n), and recursion-stack space is always O(n)."
            },
            {
              "id": "time_exponential",
              "text": "Time and stack space are exponential because each node may make two recursive calls."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "time_n_stack_h"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Total work counts all visited nodes, while stack space counts only the longest active ancestor path.",
          "details": "Depth-first recursion suspends a parent while one descendant path is explored. At any instant, the active frames correspond to one root-to-current path, so the extra call stack is proportional to tree height h. Across the complete run, however, each of the n nodes is entered and processed, giving O(n) time when per-node work is constant. A skewed tree has h = n; a balanced tree has h = O(log n). This distinction between total call count and maximum simultaneous call depth is the central accounting boundary.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "time_h_stack_n": "Height bounds one active path, not the total nodes visited. Every node contributes work even when the tree is shallow.",
            "time_n_stack_n_always": "O(n) is a valid worst-case upper bound, but it loses the decisive shape parameter. A balanced tree has h = O(log n), so only one root-to-leaf path is active.",
            "time_exponential": "The calls go to disjoint child subtrees and each real node is visited once. Branching does not imply repeated exponential states."
          }
        },
