        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Allowing negative values destroys the simple monotonic relationship between window movement and sum. That is a legality signal, not a minor edge case.",
          "details": "For nonnegative values, extending a window cannot decrease its sum and removing a leftmost value cannot increase it; those facts support a standard variable-window repair rule. With negative values, either movement may change the sum in the opposite direction, so the same local movement rule can skip the optimum. The target being positive does not restore monotonicity, and memory contiguity is unrelated to the mathematical invariant. The transfer rule is to extract sign, duplicates, bounded ranges, and ordering guarantees whenever a candidate relies on monotone progress.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "target_positive": "A positive threshold does not constrain individual values. The claim becomes valid only when the input values are also nonnegative or another invariant guarantees monotone window sums.",
            "array_contiguous": "Physical or logical contiguity identifies the output shape, not monotonicity of the aggregate. It would matter for locality costs, but it cannot justify a movement rule."
          }
        },
