            {
              "id": "alternatives",
              "text": "Add mechanisms with materially different state, preprocessing, or discard rules."
            },
            {
              "id": "annotations",
              "text": "Annotate each candidate's required preconditions and coarse time-space model."
            },
            {
              "id": "handoff",
              "text": "Hand the normalized set to legality rejection and comparative selection."
            }
          ],
          "canonicalOrder": [
            "baseline",
            "alternatives",
            "annotations",
            "handoff"
          ],
          "scoringMethod": "adjacent_relations"
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "ordering",
          "maxPoints": 3
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "A reference anchors semantics, mechanism-diverse alternatives expose choices, and explicit annotations make later rejection and comparison possible.",
          "details": "Candidate generation is neither unstructured brainstorming nor final selection. The simple baseline clarifies the relation being computed. Alternatives should then differ in the information they build or the invariant they exploit, not merely in syntax. Each candidate is incomplete until its preconditions and coarse costs are attached; otherwise later analysis must guess why it works. The final handoff deliberately leaves legality and ranking unresolved. Adjacent-relation scoring captures that alternatives depend on a known contract and evaluation depends on normalized annotations. The transfer rule is to keep this boundary in any domain: generation ends with normalized candidates, while rejection and ranking remain separate decisions."
        },
