          "reason": "The useful candidate set spans distinct cost and information mechanisms. Control-flow syntax variants do not create different strategy trade-offs.",
          "details": "Candidate generation should expose alternatives whose preprocessing, per-query work, memory, or information requirements differ. Direct scanning pays for each range; prefix sums pay one global preprocessing cost and constant work per query; offline memoization of a small known query set occupies a narrower middle ground. A for loop and while loop implement the same scan mechanism, while immediately keeping only the familiar prefix strategy hides the decision boundary. The transfer rule is to generate one representative per materially different mechanism, together with its preconditions, rather than one representative per coding style.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
