      {
        "id": "ssc-justify-hash-membership-justification",
        "prompt": "An immutable API allowlist must answer a high volume of exact token checks. The response is only allow or deny, order is irrelevant, and O(n) extra memory fits. Which justification for a hash set is strongest?",
        "constraints": [
          "Many exact membership queries occur.",
          "Arbitrary output order is acceptable.",
          "O(n) extra memory fits.",
          "Expected-time guarantees are acceptable unless stated otherwise."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "hash_complete",
              "text": "Presence-only queries need stored keys; build the set in expected O(n), answer in expected O(1), use O(n) space, and reconsider if ordered output or deterministic bounds are required."
            },
            {
              "id": "hash_fast",
              "text": "Use a hash set because lookup structures are designed for membership, usually run in constant time, and are the standard answer for repeated key queries."
            },
            {
              "id": "hash_linear",
              "text": "Build a hash set because the complete workload is expected O(n + q), which is asymptotically better than scanning and therefore settles the choice by itself."
