      {
        "id": "ssc-justify-prefix-range-formula",
        "prompt": "A static array uses prefix sums P where P[i] is the sum of the first i elements. Which explanation correctly justifies answering range [l, r] as P[r + 1] - P[l]?",
        "constraints": [
          "P[k] sums the first k elements.",
          "The array is static during queries.",
          "Ranges use inclusive endpoints [l, r]."
        ],
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "memorized_formula",
              "text": "Use P[r + 1] - P[l] because it is the standard prefix-sum formula; the conventional notation is enough to settle indexing and correctness."
            },
            {
              "id": "cancellation",
              "text": "Both prefixes contain every contribution before l, so subtraction cancels that shared part and leaves l through r; one O(n) build supports O(1) queries until updates occur."
            },
            {
              "id": "prefix_fast",
              "text": "The formula is correct because it performs one subtraction in constant time, making it faster than visiting the elements in the requested range."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "cancellation"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "The cancellation invariant proves the range formula, while the lifecycle statement explains its cost and static-data boundary.",
          "details": "With a half-open prefix convention, P[k] stores the sum of indices 0 through k-1. P[r+1] and P[l] share every contribution before l, and subtracting removes exactly that shared prefix, leaving the requested inclusive range. The stored representation costs O(n) to build and remains valid only while the underlying values do not change or are maintained consistently. Constant-time subtraction is the consequence, not the proof. The transfer rule is to state the representation invariant and derive the query operation from it rather than quoting a memorized formula.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "memorized_formula": "A formula without its indexing convention is vulnerable to off-by-one errors and does not explain why it works. It becomes acceptable shorthand only when the invariant is already fixed and shared.",
            "prefix_fast": "Execution speed does not establish semantic correctness. Subtraction is useful only because the representation makes unwanted contributions cancel exactly."
          }
        },
