      {
        "id": "ssc-transfer-sorted-shipment-pair",
        "prompt": "Shipment weights are already sorted. The system must determine whether two distinct shipments exactly fill capacity C. Which prior strategy-selection rule transfers most directly?",
        "constraints": [
          "Weights are already sorted.",
          "Two distinct shipments are required.",
          "Only existence is requested."
        ],
        "difficulty": "introductory",
        "interaction": {
          "type": "choice",
          "selectionMode": "single",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "binary_each_only",
              "text": "For every shipment, binary-search its complement in the remaining sorted weights; this is the only strategy that should exploit the order."
            },
            {
              "id": "hash_required",
              "text": "Build a hash set because pair-sum tasks require complement lookup, even when the weights are already sorted and can support ordered elimination."
            },
            {
              "id": "ordered_endpoints",
              "text": "Use opposite-end pointers; a sum below C eliminates the current lightest shipment, and a sum above C eliminates the current heaviest."
            }
          ],
          "acceptedOptionIds": [
            "ordered_endpoints"
          ]
        },
        "scoringContract": {
          "type": "choice",
          "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
        },
        "feedback": {
          "reason": "Sorted order makes the sum comparison a safe endpoint-elimination rule, so the pair-sum invariant transfers directly.",
          "details": "The logistics vocabulary changes none of the structural facts: there is an ordered sequence, a two-element sum target, and an existence output. The lightest remaining item cannot reach the target with any smaller partner when the current maximum partner is still too small; the symmetric argument removes the heaviest when the sum is too large. Hashing and repeated binary search can be legal alternatives, but the existing order makes a single O(n) pointer pass with O(1) extra state especially direct. The transfer boundary is lost ordering, original-index output constraints, or a different relation than monotone sum comparison.",
          "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
            "hash_required": "Hashing is a candidate, not a universal requirement. It becomes preferable when the input is unsorted, expected-time lookup is acceptable, and extra memory fits.",
            "binary_each_only": "Repeated binary search is legal on sorted data but costs O(n log n) and duplicates searches. It becomes relevant when pointer monotonicity is unavailable or the query structure differs."
          }
        },
