            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The sequence follows the dependency expressed by the decisive boundary: Aggregate transitions are bounded by pushes plus pops, not by the worst inner-loop length per iteration.",
              "details": "Start from the output contract and the meaning of each retained entry. Aggregate transitions are bounded by pushes plus pops, not by the worst inner-loop length per iteration. That mechanism explains both the legal transition and the point at which an answer or pending state becomes final. The misconception “Sum the maximum possible pops independently for every outer iteration.” confuses an implementation clue with a correctness condition. Transfer the aggregate or potential proof only when each entry has a bounded number of constant-work transitions; count any attached scan, copy, or nonconstant work separately. Reversing or separating a required adjacent relation either uses state before it is valid, loses provenance before writing an answer, or mutates the frontier before the invariant has been restored."
