            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Components are defined relative to the graph's edge relation, and diagonal moves add edges.",
              "details": "Mapping a grid to a graph requires an explicit neighborhood contract. With four directions, diagonally touching cells have no edge; with eight directions, they do. The outer-loop component algorithm is unchanged, but it runs on a different graph and can produce a different partition. This transfer shows that component counting is stable across representations only when the state and edge definitions are preserved.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "bfs_speed": "Algorithm speed cannot change the mathematical connectivity relation; the new diagonal edges do.",
                "visited_timing": "Visited timing affects scheduling, not which transitions are legal.",
                "tree_rule": "Grid graphs can contain cycles and disconnected regions; adjacency rules define their topology."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
