              },
              "normalizedAliasesByDimension": {
                "time": {},
                "auxiliarySpace": {}
              },
              "maxPoints": 2
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "complexity",
              "maxPoints": 2
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The path still scans each vertex and edge once, while recursive depth reaches V and visited also uses O(V).",
              "details": "DFS time follows the same adjacency-list accounting as other complete traversals: O(V + E). On a path, the recursive call chain cannot unwind until it reaches the final vertex, so stack depth is V. This is why a recursive implementation can overflow even though the asymptotic algorithm is linear. An explicit stack preserves O(V) worst-case auxiliary space while moving it out of the call stack."
            },
            "taxonomy": {
