            "feedback": {
              "reason": "All vertices and edge entries are scanned once, and one BFS layer can place nearly all vertices in the queue together.",
              "details": "The star exposes the breadth-memory boundary: after expanding the center, the queue can contain V−1 leaves. That gives O(V) frontier space in addition to O(V) visited state. Time remains O(V + E). BFS is not universally faster or cheaper than DFS; it has the decisive shortest-unweighted-path property, but its frontier width can be large."
            },
            "taxonomy": {
