            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Heap order alone does not guarantee stable ordering among equal keys, so the required tie rule must be encoded in the key.",
              "details": "Equal-priority entries may exchange positions during sift operations because the heap invariant treats them as equivalent. A composite key such as (priority, arrival sequence) turns the product’s tie rule into part of the ordering contract. Backing-array positions are transient implementation state, not stable provenance. If tie order is irrelevant, the extra field can be omitted; when it is material, it must be explicit before insertion.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "heap_stability_assumption": "Ordinary binary heaps do not promise FIFO stability for equal keys. Equal elements can move during repair.",
                "array_position_rank": "Heap positions change after pushes and pops, so position cannot represent permanent arrival order.",
                "full_sort_after_peek": "Repeated sorting is unnecessary when the tie-break can be represented directly in the priority key."
              }
            },
