            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Insertion classifies intervals from endpoint order: a gap separates X, while Y is contained inside the old range.",
              "details": "For X, old.end <= X.start, so the old interval belongs to the before phase. For Y, Y.start < old.end and old.start < Y.end, so they overlap and the merged new interval would expand to [0, 9). No duration threshold appears in either decision. This counterexample prevents an insertion implementation from choosing phases based on whether the new interval is shorter, longer, or 'large enough.' Length can affect output measure but not membership in the overlap region.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "long_old_overlaps_both": "The old interval excludes 9 and ends before X begins at 10. Duration cannot cross a positional gap.",
                "short_y_disjoint": "Y lies entirely inside [0, 9), so even a short duration has a non-empty intersection."
