              "details": "Even if binary search locates an approximate insertion position in O(log n), producing the normalized output can require traversing and copying O(n) intervals, and the new range may absorb a long contiguous overlap region. Therefore the end-to-end time bound is O(n). Excluding returned output storage, the algorithm keeps only an index and the current merged new bounds, so extra space is O(1). If the list is mutated in place with expensive shifts, the concrete cost still has a linear lower bound; a different persistent data structure would need a separately declared model."
