          {
            "id": "algorithms-intervals-insert-010",
            "prompt": "The union is stored as sweep events rather than as a sorted list of disjoint intervals. An old half-open interval ends at t and the new interval starts at t. What must an insertion update preserve?",
            "constraints": [
              "Back-to-back booking boundaries are non-conflicting.",
              "The event structure exposes intermediate active counts."
            ],
            "difficulty": "advanced",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "end_before_start_event_update",
                  "text": "Apply an end-before-start equal-time rule and update the event structure; the list-based three-phase scan cannot be reused unchanged."
                },
                {
                  "id": "arbitrary_tie",
                  "text": "Insert both events in any order because equal timestamps cancel."
                },
                {
                  "id": "sort_by_interval_length",
                  "text": "Order the two intervals by length before adding their events."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "end_before_start_event_update"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Changing representation changes the update mechanics, but it must preserve the same half-open equality semantics through an explicit event tie rule.",
              "details": "The three-phase insertion algorithm relies on a materialized start-sorted, disjoint interval sequence. An event multiset has different state and update operations, so reusing the scan would be a category error. To preserve the same booking semantics, the event representation must process release before acquisition at t when intermediate counts are meaningful. A grouped delta approach may be valid only with a proof for the exact query. This item marks the transfer boundary: endpoint semantics are reusable, while representation-specific mechanics remain separate.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "arbitrary_tie": "Intermediate active state can change with order, producing a false conflict or an unintended merged boundary.",
                "sort_by_interval_length": "Duration does not define chronological event order or endpoint inclusion. Equal or unequal lengths can appear anywhere on the timeline, so length sorting cannot preserve insertion phases."
              }
            },
