            "feedback": {
              "reason": "End-before-start lets the active count reach zero at the half-open handoff, exposing the boundary between adjacent bookings.",
              "details": "A direct start-sorted merge is simpler for this batch, but an event representation can reconstruct the same policy only if its equal-time transitions are defined. For [1, 3) and [3, 5), processing the end at 3 closes the first range before the start opens the second. Processing start first keeps active positive and produces one combined range. If the product instead wanted pure set-union compression, joining adjacent ranges could be acceptable, but the change must be explicit. The event tie is therefore part of semantic equivalence, not a sorting implementation detail.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
