              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "A sweep needs a single chronological order over every start and end event, which cannot be inferred from interval end order alone.",
              "details": "Consider [0, 100) and [50, 60). End sorting processes the shorter interval first even though the long interval's start at 0 is the earliest event. Emitting both events of each interval in that order would process time 50 before time 0 and corrupt the prefix state. The correct procedure materializes or otherwise merges all endpoint events by coordinate. End sorting can still be useful inside a different algorithm, such as a heap of active releases or an earliest-finish greedy proof, but it is not the sweep's chronological axis.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "end_sort_too_expensive": "Both are O(n log n) comparison sorts up to a constant factor. The failure is the missing chronological event order.",
                "intervals_have_no_starts": "The starts remain stored, but processing interval records in end order interleaves event times incorrectly."
              }
            },
