            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Concurrency counts simultaneous intervals; duration controls persistence but not the number of active records.",
              "details": "During [5.2, 5.8), both short intervals contribute, so the event prefix reaches two. The long-interval case has only one +1 before its -1, so the prefix never exceeds one. A sweep makes the distinction explicit: peak is the maximum prefix sum, not the width between events. Length may matter for union measure or total resource-hours, but it cannot replace active-count accumulation. This counterexample prevents a duration-based ranking from being mistaken for overlap intensity.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "long_peak_greater": "Duration affects how long demand persists, not how many intervals are active simultaneously.",
                "same_length_rule": "Peak concurrency is a count at an instant and requires no duration normalization."
