            "id": "alg-linked-list-sentinel-03-empty-and-head-deletion",
            "difficulty": "introductory"
          },
          {
            "prompt": "Two implementations are identical except one dummy stores -1 and the other stores 999. Both return dummy.next after constructing the same data-node chain. Under a sentinel-excluding contract, how do their logical outputs compare?",
            "constraints": [
              "Both functions return dummy.next.",
              "The public list model excludes sentinels."
            ],
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "same_output",
                  "text": "They are the same logical list because dummy.value is outside the returned chain."
                },
                {
                  "id": "different_first_value",
                  "text": "One result begins with -1 and the other with 999."
                },
                {
                  "id": "invalid_duplicate",
                  "text": "A dummy payload must be unique among all data payloads or the list is invalid."
                },
                {
                  "id": "different_identities",
                  "text": "Every data node receives a new identity based on dummy.value."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "same_output"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The returned reference bypasses the sentinel, so its payload does not participate in the logical sequence.",
              "details": "A sentinel's payload is often arbitrary because the algorithm uses only its stable identity and next field. Returning dummy.next exposes the data-node chain, which is stated to be identical in both implementations. A sentinel value need not be unique because node identity—not payload—is used to distinguish it internally. The conclusion changes only if the representation exposes the sentinel as a logical member.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "different_first_value": "That would be true only if the function returned dummy rather than dummy.next.",
                "different_identities": "A predecessor payload does not replace or re-identify successor node objects.",
                "invalid_duplicate": "Sentinel correctness does not depend on a payload value absent from the data domain when identity is available."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "simplify_head_boundary_with_sentinel",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "dummy_head",
              "problemArchetypeId": "remove_or_rewire_node"
            },
            "id": "alg-linked-list-sentinel-04-scaffolding-not-output",
            "difficulty": "introductory"
          },
          {
            "prompt": "dummy.next = head. Starting with prev = dummy, a routine follows prev = prev.next k times so prev becomes the predecessor of zero-based position k. No auxiliary index exists. What are the time and extra-space costs?",
            "constraints": [
              "The list is singly linked and acyclic.",
              "Only dummy, head, and k are initially available."
            ],
            "interaction": {
              "type": "complexity",
              "checkedDimensions": [
                "time",
                "extra_space"
              ],
              "availableValuesByDimension": {
                "time": [
                  "O(1)",
                  "O(log k)",
                  "O(k)",
                  "O(k^2)"
                ],
                "extra_space": [
                  "O(1)",
                  "O(k)"
                ]
              },
              "acceptedValuesByDimension": {
                "time": [
                  "O(k)"
                ],
                "extra_space": [
                  "O(1)"
                ]
              },
              "normalizedAliasesByDimension": {
                "time": {},
                "extra_space": {}
              },
              "maxPoints": 2
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "complexity",
              "maxPoints": 2
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The sentinel gives a convenient starting predecessor but every later predecessor is still reached by sequential next traversal.",
              "details": "dummy makes position zero have a predecessor at zero traversal cost. For position k, prev follows k links from dummy, so time is O(k). Only prev and a counter are needed, giving O(1) extra space. An external array or map of node references could provide different lookup behavior, but the sentinel alone is not such an index."
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "simplify_head_boundary_with_sentinel",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "dummy_head",
              "problemArchetypeId": "remove_or_rewire_node"
            },
            "id": "alg-linked-list-sentinel-05-no-arbitrary-predecessor-access",
            "difficulty": "intermediate"
          },
          {
            "prompt": "dummy.value = 0, and the first data node D also has value 0. Which test safely distinguishes the sentinel from D inside the routine?",
            "constraints": [
              "Zero is a legal data payload.",
              "The routine retains the exact dummy reference it allocated."
            ],
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "reference_to_dummy",
                  "text": "Compare the node reference with the stored dummy reference."
                },
                {
                  "id": "value_not_zero",
                  "text": "Treat every node with value 0 as the sentinel."
                },
                {
                  "id": "next_value",
                  "text": "Compare the values of their successor nodes."
                },
                {
                  "id": "position_guess",
                  "text": "Assume whichever zero-valued node is visited first is always logical data."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "reference_to_dummy"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The sentinel is one specific object, so its stored reference remains decisive even when payload values overlap.",
              "details": "Using a magic payload to identify a sentinel restricts the legal data domain and fails as soon as a data node contains that value. The routine already owns the dummy reference and can compare object identity directly. Successor values and visitation guesses do not establish role or identity. An explicit tagged node type could also work, but that is a different representation contract.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "value_not_zero": "This misclassifies legitimate zero-valued data nodes and confuses payload with identity.",
                "position_guess": "Traversal position can change during rewiring and is not a robust identity criterion.",
                "next_value": "Successor payloads do not identify the current node object or its sentinel role."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "simplify_head_boundary_with_sentinel",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "dummy_head",
              "problemArchetypeId": "remove_or_rewire_node"
            },
            "id": "alg-linked-list-sentinel-06-equal-value-distinct-node",
            "difficulty": "intermediate"
          },
          {
            "prompt": "prev may be dummy or a data node, and prev.next is the node to delete. No other reference preserves the successor. Order the transition while keeping prev ready to examine the next candidate.",
            "constraints": [
              "prev.next is non-null and is the selected victim.",
              "Consecutive candidates may also need deletion."
            ],
            "interaction": {
              "type": "ordering",
              "elements": [
                {
                  "id": "capture_victim",
                  "text": "Set victim = prev.next."
                },
                {
                  "id": "capture_successor",
                  "text": "Set successor = victim.next."
                },
                {
                  "id": "bypass",
                  "text": "Set prev.next = successor."
                },
                {
                  "id": "keep_prev",
                  "text": "Keep prev unchanged for the next candidate."
                }
              ],
              "canonicalOrder": [
                "capture_victim",
                "capture_successor",
                "bypass",
                "keep_prev"
              ],
              "scoringMethod": "adjacent_relations"
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "ordering",
              "maxPoints": 3
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The victim and successor are captured before the predecessor link changes, and deletion leaves prev at the same predecessor.",
              "details": "The sequence is identical whether prev is dummy or a data node, which is the sentinel's benefit. Capturing successor before bypassing prevents loss of the remaining chain. Keeping prev unchanged is essential when consecutive nodes may be deleted; advancing to victim would move into an unlinked node and skip the new prev.next candidate."
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "simplify_head_boundary_with_sentinel",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "dummy_head",
              "problemArchetypeId": "remove_or_rewire_node"
            },
            "id": "alg-linked-list-sentinel-07-delete-order-with-dummy-prev",
            "difficulty": "advanced"
          },
          {
            "prompt": "Select all tasks where a sentinel directly removes a material head or first-attachment special case under the stated contracts.",
            "constraints": [
              "Sentinel usefulness means it removes a real branch or missing-anchor condition.",
              "The sentinel remains outside the logical result."
            ],
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "multiple",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "find_middle_readonly",
                  "text": "Find a middle node in a read-only traversal with a fixed even-length convention."
                },
                {
                  "id": "build_merged_output",
                  "text": "Build a merged output when the first selected node is not known in advance."
                },
                {
                  "id": "remove_matching_heads",
                  "text": "Remove matching nodes when the original head may be deleted repeatedly."
                },
                {
                  "id": "detect_cycle_boolean",
                  "text": "Detect whether next traversal enters a cycle without mutating links."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "remove_matching_heads",
                "build_merged_output"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Deletion needs a uniform predecessor and merge construction needs a stable initial tail; the read-only traversal tasks do not have those boundary problems.",
              "details": "For repeated head deletion, dummy acts as the predecessor that remains before the changing logical head. For merge construction, dummy is an anchor whose next becomes the first output node. Cycle detection and middle finding rely on traversal identity or phase; adding a sentinel does not supply their proof and can shift initialization if used carelessly.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
