          {
            "id": "alg-mg-vector-negative-quarter-turn",
            "prompt": "A vector (x,y) is rotated by k=-1 quarter-turns under the convention that one positive turn maps (x,y) to (-y,x). What is the result for (2,1)?",
            "constraints": [
              "Quarter-turn count is normalized modulo 4.",
              "The language remainder for negative values may be negative."
            ],
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "clockwise_result",
                  "text": "(1,-2), because -1 normalizes to 3 positive quarter-turns."
                },
                {
                  "id": "positive_one_result",
                  "text": "(-1,2), because the sign of k can be discarded."
                },
                {
                  "id": "unchanged",
                  "text": "(2,1), because a negative remainder is treated as zero turns."
                },
                {
                  "id": "slope_rotation",
                  "text": "Compute the slope, subtract 90, and reconstruct the vector from that number alone."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "clockwise_result"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Normalizing -1 into [0,4) gives 3, which is equivalent to one clockwise quarter-turn and maps (2,1) to (1,-2).",
              "details": "Repeated coordinate transforms form a four-state cycle. The canonical count is ((k%4)+4)%4 when % may be negative. Applying the transform three times gives (2,1)→(-1,2)→(-2,-1)→(1,-2). Dropping the sign changes the rotation direction, while slope alone cannot preserve vector magnitude or handle vertical directions.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "positive_one_result": "That is the result of +1 quarter-turn, not -1.",
                "unchanged": "A negative turn is a valid inverse transformation, not a no-op.",
                "slope_rotation": "Slope loses magnitude and is undefined for vertical vectors; the coordinate transform is exact and complete."
              }
            },
            "difficulty": "foundational-transfer",
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "represent_geometric_relation_with_vectors",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "foundations",
              "patternVariantId": "coordinate_transform",
              "problemArchetypeId": "normalize_coordinates"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "alg-mg-vector-orientation-predicate-order",
            "prompt": "Order the conceptual steps for classifying C relative to the directed relation A→B using vectors.",
            "constraints": [
              "Coordinates are exact integers in a type wide enough for the determinant.",
              "Repeated A and B must remain an explicit degenerate result."
            ],
            "interaction": {
              "type": "ordering",
              "elements": [
                {
                  "id": "form_baseline",
                  "text": "Form u=B-A."
                },
                {
                  "id": "check_zero_baseline",
                  "text": "If u=(0,0), report that the directed relation has no direction."
                },
                {
                  "id": "form_candidate",
                  "text": "Form v=C-A from the same origin."
                },
                {
                  "id": "compute_cross",
                  "text": "Compute cross(u,v)."
                },
                {
                  "id": "interpret_sign",
                  "text": "Interpret positive, negative, or zero as left, right, or collinear under the stated convention."
                }
              ],
              "canonicalOrder": [
                "form_baseline",
                "check_zero_baseline",
                "form_candidate",
                "compute_cross",
                "interpret_sign"
              ],
              "scoringMethod": "adjacent_relations"
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "ordering",
              "maxPoints": 4
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The baseline must be represented and validated before its cross product is interpreted as a directional predicate.",
              "details": "B-A establishes the directed relation. If it is zero, no left/right line direction exists, so continuing would turn a degenerate algebraic zero into a false collinearity claim. C-A then supplies the comparison vector from the same origin, and the exact cross-product sign gives the classification. This order transfers to half-plane clipping and polygon winding tests, with their own endpoint and numeric boundaries."
            },
