              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Inconsistent exact-integer signs are a strong signal of intermediate overflow, including overflow before a later cast.",
              "details": "The determinant combines differences and products whose ranges can be much larger than the original coordinates. Every operation must be evaluated in a type that covers its mathematical result. An epsilon cannot repair a corrupted integer, and slope division replaces overflow with rounding and zero-denominator problems. Explicit range analysis or checked arithmetic turns the hidden failure into a precise contract.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "add_epsilon": "Epsilon addresses approximate floating boundaries, not integer wraparound.",
                "switch_to_slope": "Division loses exactness and introduces vertical-line failures without proving the quotient is safe.",
                "ignore_large_cases": "The input domain is part of correctness; implementation-dependent signs are not a valid geometric result."
