          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-choose-recursive-state-linear-suffix-needs-no-visited-set",
            "prompt": "countFrom(i) returns the number of elements in a[i:] and makes exactly one recursive call, countFrom(i + 1),\nuntil i == len(a). Which state design is justified?",
            "constraints": [
              "The function makes exactly one recursive call for every non-terminal state.",
              "The index strictly increases by one and the array does not change."
            ],
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "index_only",
                  "text": "Use only i; no visited set is needed."
                },
                {
                  "id": "index_and_visited",
                  "text": "Use i plus a set of visited indices because recursive code otherwise revisits states exponentially."
                },
                {
                  "id": "entire_prefix_history",
                  "text": "Pass every previously visited index so the child knows recursion is progressing."
                },
                {
                  "id": "memo_by_index_required",
                  "text": "Memoize every index because every recursive function requires dynamic programming."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "index_only"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "One strictly advancing child produces a chain of unique suffix states, so i is sufficient and memo state is redundant.",
              "details": "Recursion does not imply overlapping subproblems. The state graph here is i -> i + 1 -> ... -> len(a), with exactly one visit to each index. The index completely identifies the remaining suffix, and neither a visited set nor a memo table changes the asymptotic work. Additional state becomes justified when branching transitions can converge on the same state, as in naive Fibonacci or many grid paths. The transfer boundary is therefore call-graph overlap, not the presence of recursive syntax.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "index_and_visited": "This call graph is a single chain. Each increasing index occurs once, so a visited set records facts the control flow already guarantees and does not prevent any repeated work.",
                "entire_prefix_history": "The ordered history is derivable from the start and current i and is irrelevant to the suffix count. It makes state grow without changing the answer.",
                "memo_by_index_required": "Memoization is useful when the same state can be reached through multiple paths. Here each state has only one predecessor in the actual call chain, so no overlap exists."
              }
            },
