            "feedback": {
              "reason": "A two-position transition must stop for both zero and one remaining element; neither state contains a complete pair.",
              "details": "The progress measure is the number of elements remaining, len(a) - i. Each recursive call reduces it by two. Depending on the initial parity, the measure reaches 0 or 1. Both are terminal because the recursive operation requires two elements. An equality check for only the empty suffix reflects the false heuristic that base cases are exclusively empty inputs. The robust rule is to encode the operation's precondition: recurse only while at least one complete pair remains.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "i_equal_length": "For an odd-length array, i advances from the last valid index to a state with one unpaired element; equality with len(a) is not guaranteed before a pair read would be invalid.",
                "i_equal_last_index": "This covers the one-leftover case but misses the exact empty suffix reached from even lengths. The terminal region must cover both.",
                "array_empty_initially": "Termination depends on the current suffix, not solely on the initial input. Non-empty arrays still reach a suffix with fewer than two elements."
              }
