          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-base-case-and-result-contract-define-contract-before-code",
            "prompt": "Order the reasoning steps for defining a recursive function whose result will be consumed by its caller.",
            "constraints": [
              "The function returns a value rather than mutating an external accumulator as its primary result.",
              "The valid input domain and terminal shapes must be stated explicitly before implementation."
            ],
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "ordering",
              "elements": [
                {
                  "id": "state_meaning",
                  "text": "State exactly what f(state) returns for every valid state."
                },
                {
                  "id": "terminal_states",
                  "text": "Identify all terminal states in the declared input domain."
                },
                {
                  "id": "terminal_returns",
                  "text": "Assign terminal return values that satisfy the stated meaning."
                },
                {
                  "id": "recursive_assumption",
                  "text": "Assume each strictly smaller call returns that same meaning for its own state."
                },
                {
                  "id": "current_result",
                  "text": "Use those returned values to produce the current state's promised result."
                }
              ],
              "canonicalOrder": [
                "state_meaning",
                "terminal_states",
                "terminal_returns",
                "recursive_assumption",
                "current_result"
              ],
              "scoringMethod": "adjacent_relations"
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "ordering",
              "maxPoints": 4
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "A recursive contract is coherent only when terminal and non-terminal calls return the same semantic kind of answer.",
              "details": "The return meaning is the anchor. Without it, a base value such as 0, 1, false, or an empty collection can\nlook plausible while representing the wrong thing. Once the valid domain is known, every terminal shape\nmust receive an answer that satisfies that meaning. The recursive assumption is then legitimate: a smaller\ncall provides exactly the kind of value the current frame expects. The final step is not arbitrary syntax;\nit must transform child answers into the current answer. This sequence transfers across numeric recursion,\nrecursive structures, and result generation. Choosing parameters and proving progress are separate\ndecisions that must be checked after the contract is stated."
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "define_recursive_base_and_return_contract",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "recursive_decomposition",
              "problemArchetypeId": "generate_recursive_result"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-base-case-and-result-contract-all-even-empty-suffix",
            "prompt": "allEvenFrom(i) returns whether every value in a[i:] is even. The array is immutable, and i may equal len(a).\nSelect all statements that must hold when i == len(a).",
            "constraints": [
              "allEvenFrom(i) is defined for 0 <= i <= len(a).",
              "The result is a pure Boolean value."
            ],
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "multiple",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "return_true",
                  "text": "Return true."
                },
                {
                  "id": "do_not_read",
                  "text": "Do not access a[i]."
                },
                {
                  "id": "return_false",
                  "text": "Return false because the suffix contains no even number."
                },
                {
                  "id": "inspect_previous",
                  "text": "Inspect a[i - 1] to decide the empty suffix."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "return_true",
                "do_not_read"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The empty suffix satisfies the universal predicate and contains no element that may be read.",
              "details": "allEvenFrom(i) is a universal statement over the remaining suffix. At i == len(a), there is no counterexample,\nso the result is true. This choice is also algebraically coherent: a non-terminal frame can return\nisEven(a[i]) AND allEvenFrom(i + 1), and true is the neutral value for AND. Reading a[i] at the terminal\nstate would violate the bounds, while reading a previous element would violate the meaning of the current\nsuffix. The transfer boundary distinguishes universal folds from existential ones: anyEvenFrom over an\nempty suffix returns false because false is the neutral value for OR.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "return_false": "The contract asks whether every element satisfies a condition, not whether at least one even element exists. The universal statement over an empty suffix is true, which also makes true the neutral value for recursive AND.",
                "inspect_previous": "The state i == len(a) represents no remaining elements. Looking backward reprocesses data outside the current subproblem and makes the result depend on history rather than the suffix contract."
              },
              "omittedCorrectExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "return_true": "True preserves the meaning of “every remaining element is even” and is neutral under AND.",
                "do_not_read": "No array element belongs to the empty suffix, so a correct terminal path performs no element access."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "define_recursive_base_and_return_contract",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "base_case_recognition",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_from_smaller_input"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-base-case-and-result-contract-sum-to-product-contract-transfer",
            "prompt": "productFrom(i) is created from a correct recursive sumFrom(i). Both process the immutable suffix a[i:].\nWhich contract change is necessary for productFrom?",
            "constraints": [
              "Array values are ordinary finite numbers.",
              "Overflow behavior is outside this item."
            ],
            "difficulty": "advanced",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "one_and_multiply",
                  "text": "Return 1 for the empty suffix and return a[i] * productFrom(i + 1) otherwise."
                },
                {
                  "id": "zero_and_multiply",
                  "text": "Return 0 for the empty suffix and multiply on the way back."
                },
                {
                  "id": "one_and_add",
                  "text": "Return 1 for the empty suffix but keep addition in the recursive case."
                },
                {
                  "id": "reuse_sum_unchanged",
                  "text": "Keep return 0 and addition because the recursive state is unchanged."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "one_and_multiply"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The empty result and the combiner must change together so every frame returns a product.",
              "details": "The suffix state and progress rule are reusable, but the return contract is not. For multiplication, the\nempty suffix must contribute the multiplicative identity 1, and each frame multiplies its current value by\nthe product of the remainder. Keeping the sum base or combiner would make terminal and recursive returns\ndisagree with the promised quantity. This is the transfer rule for folds: decomposition can stay stable\nwhile the identity and combining operation are selected from the requested algebra. It does not mean every\noperation has an empty identity; maximum over an unrestricted empty set needs a different domain or\nsentinel contract.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "zero_and_multiply": "Zero is not neutral under multiplication. Every non-empty product would collapse to zero when the recursion reaches the terminal suffix.",
                "one_and_add": "The terminal identity alone is insufficient. Addition composes suffix sums, not suffix products, so the non-terminal result would violate the new contract.",
                "reuse_sum_unchanged": "The state decomposition can remain unchanged while the return algebra changes. Reusing the sum contract would compute a different quantity."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "define_recursive_base_and_return_contract",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "divide_and_combine",
              "problemArchetypeId": "generate_recursive_result"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-base-case-and-result-contract-singleton-maximum-contract",
            "prompt": "maxFrom(i) returns the maximum in the non-empty suffix a[i:] for an array that may contain negative values.\nWhich base case preserves that contract?",
            "constraints": [
              "len(a) > 0.",
              "The caller supplies 0 <= i < len(a)."
            ],
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "singleton_value",
                  "text": "If i == len(a) - 1, return a[i]."
                },
                {
                  "id": "empty_zero",
                  "text": "If i == len(a), return 0."
                },
                {
                  "id": "singleton_zero",
                  "text": "If i == len(a) - 1, return 0."
                },
                {
                  "id": "no_base",
                  "text": "No terminal return is needed because max can be computed while unwinding."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "singleton_value"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "A singleton suffix has a fully determined maximum even though it is not empty.",
              "details": "The declared subproblem is deliberately non-empty. Its smallest valid instance contains one element, and\nthe only value satisfying “maximum of this suffix” is that element. Introducing an empty state with zero\nwould both escape the domain and corrupt negative inputs. This distinction is important when an operation\nhas no safe universal identity. A base case is a terminal state whose answer is known directly; it is not a\nsynonym for an empty collection. The same contract pattern applies to minimum, last-node values, and leaf\nsummaries.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "empty_zero": "The function contract excludes the empty suffix, and zero is not a valid neutral maximum for all-negative data. It would introduce a value that is not in the suffix.",
                "singleton_zero": "A singleton maximum is the element itself. Returning zero changes the meaning and fails whenever the final value is not zero.",
                "no_base": "Unwinding can combine values only after a smallest call returns a valid maximum. Without a terminal return, the recursive chain has no result to propagate."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "define_recursive_base_and_return_contract",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "base_case_recognition",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_from_smaller_input"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-base-case-and-result-contract-uniform-contract-changing-state",
            "prompt": "containsFrom(i, target) returns whether target appears in a[i:]. It calls containsFrom(i + 1, target)\nwhen a[i] is not the target. Which property must remain identical across frames?",
            "constraints": [
              "target is immutable during the call chain.",
              "containsFrom is defined through i == len(a)."
            ],
            "difficulty": "introductory",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "return_meaning",
                  "text": "Each frame returns a Boolean answering whether target occurs in its own remaining suffix."
                },
                {
                  "id": "index_value",
                  "text": "Each frame must receive the same value of i."
                },
                {
                  "id": "suffix_contents",
                  "text": "Each frame must cover exactly the same set of array positions."
                },
                {
                  "id": "local_branch",
                  "text": "Every frame must take the same branch before returning."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "return_meaning"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The contract stays constant while i changes which suffix the contract applies to.",
              "details": "A parent can trust a child's Boolean only because every call uses the same semantic promise. The index is\nallowed—and required—to change, since it names the remaining suffix. Some frames may find the target\nlocally, others may delegate, and the terminal frame may return false, but all results answer the same kind\nof question. Confusing identical contract with identical state makes recursion impossible to shrink. This\nprinciple transfers to intervals, linked nodes, remaining counts, and recursive children.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "index_value": "The index must change to identify a smaller suffix. Reusing the same index would repeat the same element and prevent progress.",
                "suffix_contents": "The child intentionally covers fewer positions. What stays stable is the meaning of the answer, not the concrete extent of the subproblem.",
                "local_branch": "Different data can make one frame return immediately and another recurse. Control flow may vary while the return contract remains uniform."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "define_recursive_base_and_return_contract",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "recursive_decomposition",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_from_smaller_input"
            }
