          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-base-case-and-result-contract-subtract-two-total-domain",
            "prompt": "steps(n) is defined for every non-negative integer and returns the number of times 2 must be subtracted\nuntil the value becomes non-positive. Which definition makes the recursive contract total?",
            "constraints": [
              "n starts as an integer with n >= 0.",
              "The stopping condition is value <= 0, not value == 0."
            ],
            "difficulty": "advanced",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "nonpositive_base",
                  "text": "If n <= 0 return 0; otherwise return 1 + steps(n - 2)."
                },
                {
                  "id": "zero_only",
                  "text": "If n == 0 return 0; otherwise return 1 + steps(n - 2)."
                },
                {
                  "id": "one_only",
                  "text": "If n == 1 return 0; otherwise return 1 + steps(n - 2)."
                },
                {
                  "id": "negative_is_error",
                  "text": "If n < 0 raise an error because recursive calls may not leave the original domain."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "nonpositive_base"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Subtracting two reaches either 0 or -1, so the base contract must cover both non-positive terminal states.",
              "details": "A base condition is not sufficient merely because it exists. It must cover every terminal state reachable\nfrom valid inputs. Even values descend to 0; odd values descend to -1. Returning 0 additional steps for all\nn <= 0 preserves the meaning of the function, while each positive frame adds the subtraction it performs.\nA zero-only base would make the recursion non-total over half of its declared domain. The transfer rule is\nto trace residue classes, branch outcomes, and boundary crossings when selecting a terminal predicate.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "zero_only": "Odd inputs reach -1 rather than 0, so the zero-only base does not cover every reachable terminal state in the declared domain.",
                "one_only": "Even inputs reach 0, which this base does not cover. The contract needs one terminal predicate that covers both parity paths.",
                "negative_is_error": "The function's stated result is defined by stopping once the value is non-positive. Reaching -1 from an odd input is an intended terminal state, not an exceptional one."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "define_recursive_base_and_return_contract",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "base_case_recognition",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_from_smaller_input"
            }
