          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-base-case-and-result-contract-disjoint-halves-return-contract",
            "prompt": "rangeSum(lo, hi) returns the sum of a[lo:hi). For hi - lo > 1 it splits at mid, asks for the left sum and\nthe right sum, then adds them. Which statement makes the two-call recurrence contractually valid?",
            "constraints": [
              "The array is immutable.",
              "mid satisfies lo < mid < hi for non-terminal ranges."
            ],
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "disjoint_complete_parts",
                  "text": "The two child ranges are disjoint, cover the parent range exactly, and both return sums under the same contract."
                },
                {
                  "id": "two_calls_invalid",
                  "text": "A recursive contract may contain only one child call; two calls are inherently exponential and invalid."
                },
                {
                  "id": "children_return_elements",
                  "text": "Each child may return any representative element because the parent only needs a number."
                },
                {
                  "id": "overlap_is_required",
                  "text": "The child ranges must overlap so both calls confirm the same elements."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "disjoint_complete_parts"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Two calls are coherent because each returns the same semantic quantity for one non-overlapping part of the parent.",
              "details": "The parent range is exactly the union of [lo, mid) and [mid, hi), with no overlap. By the recursive\ncontract, each child returns the sum of its own range; addition therefore produces the parent's sum. The\nmere presence of two calls does not make the contract invalid or the work exponential. With balanced\ndisjoint halves, the recursion tree has linear total leaves and logarithmic depth. This item owns the return\ncontract; formal complexity derivation belongs to the stack-and-complexity unit.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "two_calls_invalid": "Multiple child calls are valid when they solve distinct required parts. Exponential work is a cost question, not a reason the return contract is incoherent.",
                "children_return_elements": "The parent expects each number to mean the sum of its child range. A matching type is not enough; the semantic return contract must match.",
                "overlap_is_required": "Overlapping sums would double-count shared elements unless the combiner explicitly corrected for them. This decomposition is correct because the halves partition the parent."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
