            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Tail recursion changes the continuation shape, but stack complexity remains runtime-dependent unless tail-call elimination is guaranteed.",
              "details": "The recursive call is the final operation, so the current frame has no pending arithmetic after the child returns. A compiler or runtime may exploit that fact by replacing the call with a jump and reusing one frame. The content contract, however, states that such optimization is not guaranteed. Under ordinary call semantics, n, acc, and return control are represented by one frame per call, giving depth O(n). The transfer rule is to separate source-level tail position from an implementation-level tail-call guarantee.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "constant_stack_by_syntax": "Tail position makes frame reuse possible, not mandatory. Without a language/runtime guarantee, ordinary calls can retain one frame per decrement.",
                "exponential_stack": "Numeric result magnitude does not determine call depth. There is one recursive child and n decrements.",
                "zero_stack": "The accumulator removes post-return arithmetic, but the function call itself still needs runtime control state unless optimized into iteration."
              }
            },
