            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The disjoint recursion tree contains O(n) total nodes, while balanced depth keeps only O(log n) frames active at once.",
              "details": "The time recurrence is T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(1). The n one-element leaves and fewer than n internal combine nodes give total O(n) work. Stack space is not the sum of frames across the whole tree: depth-first evaluation completes one branch before its sibling, so only one root-to-leaf path plus constant sibling bookkeeping is active. Balanced halving produces O(log n) depth. The result changes if each frame allocates an n-sized copy or if the split is highly unbalanced; those costs are excluded by the stated constraints."
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "account_for_recursive_call_stack",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "divide_and_combine",
              "problemArchetypeId": "divide_problem"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-stack-and-complexity-singleton-base-frame-count",
            "prompt": "maxFrom(i) returns the maximum of the non-empty suffix a[i:] in an array of length n > 0.\nIt stops at i == n - 1 and otherwise calls maxFrom(i + 1). What is the maximum number of simultaneously\nactive maxFrom frames from maxFrom(0)?",
            "constraints": [
              "The input array is non-empty.",
              "The function is never called with i == n.",
              "The runtime does not perform tail-call optimization because comparison remains after the child returns."
            ],
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "n_frames",
                  "text": "n frames, including the singleton base frame."
                },
                {
                  "id": "n_plus_one_empty",
                  "text": "n + 1 frames because every recursive function must add an empty-suffix base frame."
                },
                {
                  "id": "one_frame",
                  "text": "One frame because each call replaces the previous call."
                },
                {
                  "id": "two_power_n",
                  "text": "2^n frames because recursive maximum is exponential."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "n_frames"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The legal suffix states are i = 0 through n - 1, and all n corresponding frames are live at the deepest point.",
              "details": "The base case is the one-element suffix, not an invented empty suffix. Starting at i = 0, recursion creates frames for every valid index through n - 1. Just before the singleton returns, n frames are active. The parent frames are suspended because each still needs to compare its local element with the child's return. This directly counters the empty-only base heuristic and shows its resource consequence: stack accounting follows actual reachable states, not a universal template that adds one more empty call.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "n_plus_one_empty": "The declared domain is non-empty suffixes, and i == n - 1 already supplies the atomic answer. An empty frame is neither called nor required.",
                "one_frame": "The parent still needs its a[i] value and continuation to compare with the child maximum, so ordinary calls remain suspended until unwinding.",
                "two_power_n": "There is one child per non-terminal frame, creating a chain rather than a branching call tree."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "account_for_recursive_call_stack",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "base_case_recognition",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_from_smaller_input"
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "recursion-basics-recursion-stack-and-complexity-separate-frame-local-values",
            "prompt": "sumFrom(i) computes rest = sumFrom(i + 1) and then returns a[i] + rest.\nWhile processing a three-element suffix, which statement about rest is correct?",
            "constraints": [
              "rest is a function-local variable.",
              "No global or captured mutable variable named rest is used.",
              "The runtime uses ordinary call frames."
            ],
            "difficulty": "introductory",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "one_rest_per_frame",
                  "text": "Each active frame owns a separate rest slot that receives its own child's return value."
                },
                {
                  "id": "one_shared_rest",
                  "text": "All calls share one rest variable because the source code uses the same name."
                },
                {
                  "id": "rest_exists_only_base",
                  "text": "rest exists only in the base frame."
                },
                {
                  "id": "rest_values_identical",
                  "text": "Every rest value is identical because every call executes the same statement."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
