              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "A fixed window still uses one-out/one-in updates, but repeated categories require multiplicity-aware frequency state.",
              "details": "The boundary movement is unchanged from a sum window. What changes is the aggregate. Adding a type whose count was zero increases distinct; removing a type whose count becomes zero decreases distinct. A set alone loses multiplicity: removing one copy would erase a type that may still occur elsewhere in the window. Numeric label sums do not identify distinctness, and sorting each block discards the intended incremental update. This is the transfer rule from scalar to categorical fixed-window state.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
