              "details": "The usual O(n) proof assumes O(1) or otherwise bounded state maintenance per element entering or leaving. If each outer iteration rescans a window that can contain Θ(n) elements, the sum of scan lengths can be Θ(n²), even when left never moves backward. Rare shrinking can make this worse by keeping windows large. To recover linear or near-linear time, maintain the validity-driving statistic incrementally or use a structure with a stated update/query cost.",
