              },
              "normalizedAliasesByDimension": {
                "time": {},
                "space": {}
              },
              "maxPoints": 2
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "complexity",
              "maxPoints": 2
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Every node is dequeued once, and the queue's peak occupancy is bounded by the widest active frontier.",
              "details": "Constant work per enqueue and dequeue across `n` nodes gives `O(n)` time. The queue contains nodes from at most the current and next frontier; asymptotically its maximum size is `O(w)`. A complete tree can have `w = Θ(n)`, while a chain has `w = 1`. This differs from recursive DFS, whose live frames are `O(h)`. Both traversals can be linear in time, so the decisive auxiliary-space parameter is tree shape and the chosen frontier representation."
            },
