            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The required sum is ancestor-derived branch state, so each child should receive the current branch's next value without inheriting mutations from its sibling.",
              "details": "A root-to-current prefix is different on different branches. Passing `nextRemaining` by value makes that branch state explicit and naturally restores the parent's value when a call returns. One global mutable value leaks the left branch's subtraction into the right branch unless carefully undone. Recomputing from the root preserves correctness but repeats work, and overwriting node values corrupts the input and mixes traversal state with data. The same pattern applies to path minima, parity, constraints, and accumulated labels when the next state can be derived from the parent state and current node.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "mutate_global_remaining": "A single variable looks economical, but sibling calls share it and the second branch observes the first branch's updates. It is valid only with an explicit save-and-restore discipline and no concurrent traversal.",
                "recompute_from_root": "This can be correct if parent links exist, but it turns a local transition into repeated path scans and can produce quadratic work on a chain.",
                "store_in_node": "Embedding state in nodes avoids a parameter but destroys original values and makes reuse unsafe. It is appropriate only if destructive prefix transformation is the actual required output."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
