            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The current value must be present for leaf recording and descendant calls, then removed so the sibling branch receives only the parent prefix.",
              "details": "Append establishes the root-to-current invariant. At a leaf, snapshot the current list because the reusable buffer will later change. At an internal node, children extend the same prefix one at a time. The final removal is the backtracking step that restores exactly the state the caller supplied. Recording before append omits the leaf; removing before recursion omits the ancestor from descendants; failing to remove leaks one branch into its sibling. A `try/finally`-style restoration is useful when recursion may exit early or throw."
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "carry_state_along_tree_path",
