          {
            "id": "alg-tree-subtree-full-property-combine",
            "prompt": "A binary tree is full when every node has either zero children or two children. A helper returns whether the subtree rooted at `node` is full.\nWhich parent rule correctly derives the result from its children?",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "combine_child_validity_and_degree",
                  "text": "After obtaining both child results, return true when both subtrees are full and the current node has either zero or two children."
                },
                {
                  "id": "parent_value_only",
                  "text": "Return true when the current node value is even, regardless of child structure."
                },
                {
                  "id": "first_child_decides",
                  "text": "Return the left child's result and ignore the right subtree."
                },
                {
                  "id": "global_leaf_count",
                  "text": "Count leaves globally and return true whenever the count is even."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "combine_child_validity_and_degree"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Fullness is a subtree property: the current node is valid only when both child subtrees are valid and its own child count satisfies the local rule.",
              "details": "The recursive return captures the same property for a smaller subtree. Null may be treated as full for the child-result identity, while a non-null parent checks both returned Booleans and its local degree. Ignoring either child loses a possible violation, and a global leaf count does not characterize fullness. This local-and-child combine pattern transfers to structural predicates such as perfectness or uniform labels, with different local conditions and possibly richer returned metadata.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "parent_value_only": "Payload parity is unrelated to the structural requirement. It would be valid only for a value-based predicate rather than fullness.",
                "first_child_decides": "Either subtree can contain a one-child node, so both results are required. A one-sided return is valid only for a property intentionally defined on one designated branch.",
                "global_leaf_count": "Full trees can have different leaf-count parity, and non-full trees can have an even number of leaves. Aggregate counts do not replace the local degree invariant."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "derive_parent_result_from_subtrees",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "subtree_aggregation",
              "problemArchetypeId": "validate_tree_property"
            },
            "difficulty": "foundational",
            "constraints": [
              "The input is a proper binary tree.",
              "A leaf has zero children."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "alg-tree-subtree-bst-summary-sequence",
            "prompt": "A helper returns a summary `{isBST, minValue, maxValue}` for each subtree. Order the conceptual steps for a non-null node.",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "ordering",
              "elements": [
                {
                  "id": "define_empty_summary",
                  "text": "Use the declared valid empty-subtree summary when a child is null."
                },
                {
                  "id": "obtain_left_summary",
                  "text": "Obtain the complete summary for the left subtree."
                },
                {
                  "id": "obtain_right_summary",
                  "text": "Obtain the complete summary for the right subtree."
                },
                {
                  "id": "build_parent_summary",
                  "text": "Check both child validity flags and value bounds, then return the current subtree's validity, minimum, and maximum."
                }
              ],
              "canonicalOrder": [
                "define_empty_summary",
                "obtain_left_summary",
                "obtain_right_summary",
                "build_parent_summary"
              ],
              "scoringMethod": "adjacent_relations"
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "ordering",
              "maxPoints": 3
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The parent summary is a deterministic function of two complete child summaries and the current value, so those inputs must exist before the combine step.",
              "details": "The summary is deliberately richer than one Boolean because an ancestor needs boundary values to validate its own ordering relation. The empty summary supplies neutral bounds and a valid flag. After both child summaries return, the current node checks `left.maxValue < node.value < right.minValue` under a strict policy and propagates the overall min and max. Storing min/max in global variables would mix unrelated branches; returning them keeps each subtree's evidence attached to that subtree. A duplicate policy would change the comparison operators but not the dependency structure."
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "derive_parent_result_from_subtrees",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "subtree_aggregation",
              "problemArchetypeId": "validate_tree_property"
            },
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "constraints": [
              "The BST policy is strict: duplicate values are not allowed.",
              "Scoring uses preserved adjacent relations."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "alg-tree-subtree-edge-height-null-identity",
            "prompt": "Height is defined as the number of edges on the longest path from a node to a leaf, so a leaf has height `0`.\nWhich null-subtree return value makes `height(node) = 1 + max(height(left), height(right))` work for a leaf?",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "minus_one",
                  "text": "`-1`"
                },
                {
                  "id": "zero",
                  "text": "`0`"
                },
                {
                  "id": "one",
                  "text": "`1`"
                },
                {
                  "id": "negative_infinity",
                  "text": "Negative infinity"
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "minus_one"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "A leaf's two null children must contribute `-1`, yielding `1 + max(-1, -1) = 0`.",
              "details": "Base values depend on the measurement convention. For edge height, a null subtree is one edge below a leaf's height and therefore returns `-1`. If height counted nodes, null would return `0` and a leaf would return `1`. Negative infinity also makes the leaf equation unusable without a special case because adding one remains negative infinity. The key is not memorizing one universal base value but choosing the identity that makes the parent recurrence satisfy the declared boundary examples.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "zero": "Zero is the correct null value for node-count height, but under edge-count height it makes a leaf height one.",
                "one": "One shifts every height by two relative to the edge convention. It would require a different recurrence and boundary definition.",
                "negative_infinity": "This can represent “no path” in max computations, but the stated recurrence would never recover a finite leaf height without an extra special case."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "derive_parent_result_from_subtrees",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "subtree_aggregation",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_subtree_property"
            },
            "difficulty": "foundational",
            "constraints": [
              "Height is measured in edges.",
              "The recurrence is fixed as stated."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "alg-tree-subtree-diameter-composite-return",
            "prompt": "A one-pass helper must let its parent compute paths through the current node and must also preserve the best diameter entirely inside either child subtree.\nWhat should each call return?",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "height_and_best",
                  "text": "A constant-sized summary containing both subtree height and the best diameter found anywhere in that subtree."
                },
                {
                  "id": "height_only_no_other_state",
                  "text": "Only subtree height, with no other returned or external state."
                },
                {
                  "id": "global_nodes_list",
                  "text": "A global list of every node visited so the diameter can be reconstructed later."
                },
                {
                  "id": "parent_depth",
                  "text": "Only the depth of the current node from the root."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "height_and_best"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "The parent needs height for a path crossing itself, while the subtree's answer may already lie below it, so both facts must travel upward.",
              "details": "For each subtree, return `height` and `bestDiameter`. The parent forms a crossing candidate from the two heights and sets its best diameter to the maximum of that candidate and both child bests. This keeps all required evidence compositional and avoids a traversal-wide mutable maximum. A global maximum can also yield a correct one-pass implementation when its ownership and reset rules are explicit, but it weakens the reusable return contract. Returning height alone loses a diameter that does not pass through an ancestor.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "height_only_no_other_state": "Height is enough for the parent's crossing candidate but cannot preserve a diameter entirely inside one child. It becomes sufficient only when a separate, correctly owned global best is explicitly part of the contract.",
                "global_nodes_list": "Collecting all nodes does not directly encode path connectivity and adds unnecessary storage. A later reconstruction would need more structure and work.",
                "parent_depth": "Depth is ancestor-derived state and does not summarize descendant path lengths. It is useful for root-distance queries, not subtree diameter."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "derive_parent_result_from_subtrees",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "subtree_aggregation",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_subtree_property"
            },
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "constraints": [
              "Diameter is measured consistently in either nodes or edges; only relative combine structure matters."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "alg-tree-subtree-proper-tree-versus-dag",
            "prompt": "A pure recursive helper computes a deterministic summary from child summaries. What changes when the input may be a DAG with the same child node referenced by multiple parents?",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "memoize_shared_subtrees",
                  "text": "The return contract remains meaningful, but memoization by node identity may be required to avoid recomputing a shared subgraph; cycles would require an additional policy or rejection."
                },
                {
                  "id": "nothing_ever_changes",
                  "text": "Nothing changes: every node is still called exactly once automatically."
                },
                {
                  "id": "deduplicate_by_value",
                  "text": "Cache by node value because equal values imply equal subgraphs."
                },
                {
                  "id": "use_preorder_only",
                  "text": "Switching to preorder alone prevents repeated work on shared nodes."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "memoize_shared_subtrees"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Shared identity breaks the one-parent, one-call property of a tree even though the child-to-parent summary equation can remain valid.",
              "details": "In a proper tree, recursive descent reaches each node through one path, so each summary is naturally computed once. In a DAG, several parents can request the same node's summary. A pure function can safely memoize that result by node identity, preserving the same recurrence and reducing repeated work. Value-based caching is unsafe because equal payloads can head different subgraphs. If cycles are possible, no finite bottom-up summary exists without a defined fixed-point, cycle rejection, or other graph-specific contract.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "nothing_ever_changes": "A shared child can be reached once from each parent, so the helper may run multiple times. This statement becomes true only if the representation guarantees unique parent ownership.",
                "deduplicate_by_value": "Equal values do not imply equal descendants or identity. Cache by identity or by a proven structural key.",
                "use_preorder_only": "Action timing does not remove multiple incoming paths. Preorder may change when work occurs, but memoization or visited state addresses duplication."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "derive_parent_result_from_subtrees",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "subtree_aggregation",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_subtree_property"
            },
            "difficulty": "advanced",
            "constraints": [
