            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Returned arrays have sizes `1, 2, ..., n` along the chain, so copying them at successive parents performs a quadratic total number of element moves.",
              "details": "The recursive call count is linear, but local combine work is not constant. The node at distance `k` from the leaf copies `Θ(k)` values, and the sum over the chain is `Θ(n^2)`. If the parent needs only a count, minimum, height, or other constant-sized summary, return that summary and restore `O(n)` total time. If the full lists are required as output for every subtree, the quadratic amount of produced data is itself an output-size lower bound. The cost follows the return payload size, not just the traversal label."
