            "difficulty": "foundational",
            "constraints": [
              "The tree satisfies the declared BST ordering policy.",
              "The output must be streamed without materializing all values."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "alg-tree-choice-parent-from-child-summaries",
            "prompt": "At every node, a result must be computed from complete summaries returned by both child subtrees, and the implementation should avoid a traversal-wide mutable answer.\nWhich strategy is the direct fit?",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "postorder_returns",
                  "text": "Postorder DFS with a subtree summary returned from each child call."
                },
                {
                  "id": "preorder_global",
                  "text": "Preorder DFS with one global variable that is assumed to contain both child summaries before they are visited."
                },
                {
                  "id": "inorder_left_only",
                  "text": "Inorder DFS that combines after the left child and ignores the right child."
                },
                {
                  "id": "bfs_parent_first",
                  "text": "Top-down BFS that computes a parent summary before descendants have been processed."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "postorder_returns"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Both child summaries are prerequisites for the parent result, so child-before-parent postorder with explicit return values matches the dependency.",
              "details": "A postorder call obtains the left summary, obtains the right summary, and combines them with local node data. Returning the summary keeps evidence branch-local and reusable. A global variable can hold a whole-tree answer, but it cannot magically provide two child-specific values before traversal; it would require additional maps or phases. Reverse-level processing can also become bottom-up, but ordinary top-down BFS does not satisfy the dependency directly and usually stores more intermediate state.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "preorder_global": "Preorder reaches the parent before either child result exists. Global storage does not remove that dependency; it only hides ownership.",
                "inorder_left_only": "The right summary is still a required input, so combining after only the left child is incomplete. Inorder fits a current action between ordered children.",
                "bfs_parent_first": "A parent-first frontier has not computed descendants yet. A deliberately reversed BFS with stored levels could work, but it is not this option's top-down rule."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "choose_tree_traversal_order",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "dfs_postorder",
              "problemArchetypeId": "compute_subtree_property"
            },
            "difficulty": "foundational",
            "constraints": [
              "Each parent needs both child summaries.",
              "The summary size is bounded."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "alg-tree-choice-nearest-target-tree-and-graph",
            "prompt": "Find the minimum number of edges from the root to any node satisfying predicate `P`.\nWhich choice states both the proper-tree solution and the change required if the structure becomes a cyclic unweighted graph?",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "single",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "bfs_then_add_visited",
                  "text": "Use BFS and stop at the first dequeued matching node; for the graph, add identity-based visited marking at enqueue time."
                },
                {
                  "id": "dfs_same_for_both",
                  "text": "Use ordinary recursive DFS for both and stop at the first matching node reached."
                },
                {
                  "id": "bfs_no_visited_for_both",
                  "text": "Use BFS for both; FIFO order alone prevents graph revisits."
                },
                {
                  "id": "postorder_then_min",
                  "text": "Use postorder and accept the first matching node processed."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "bfs_then_add_visited"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "BFS explores nondecreasing edge distance, while the graph variant additionally needs discovery state to prevent cycles and duplicate enqueues.",
              "details": "In an unweighted structure, each BFS layer is one edge farther from the start. Therefore the first matching node dequeued has minimum distance. A proper tree reaches every node through one root path, so visited state is unnecessary. A graph may expose the same vertex through several paths or cycles, so mark identity on enqueue. DFS can compute a minimum after exploring all relevant branches, but its first match is not guaranteed nearest. Postorder deliberately sees deep descendants before their ancestors.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "dfs_same_for_both": "First-match DFS depends on branch order and may find a deep match before a shallow sibling. Graph DFS also needs cycle handling.",
                "bfs_no_visited_for_both": "FIFO preserves distance order but does not prevent cyclic or duplicate discovery. It is sufficient only under a proper-tree or other unique-path contract.",
                "postorder_then_min": "Postorder completion order has no shortest-distance guarantee and may process a deep match before a shallow one. It is appropriate for bottom-up subtree properties, not first-nearest stopping."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
              "primarySkillAtomId": "choose_tree_traversal_order",
              "secondarySkillAtomIds": [],
              "learningStage": "pattern_mechanics",
              "patternVariantId": "bfs_level_order",
              "problemArchetypeId": "level_order_processing"
            },
            "difficulty": "intermediate",
            "constraints": [
              "Every edge has unit cost.",
              "The predicate is evaluated in constant time."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "alg-tree-choice-order-independent-total-sum",
            "prompt": "The only required result is the sum of all node values in a proper binary tree, and no early stopping or per-level output is required. Select all traversal statements that are correct.",
            "interaction": {
              "type": "choice",
              "selectionMode": "multiple",
              "options": [
                {
                  "id": "preorder_valid",
                  "text": "A one-visit preorder DFS can compute the sum."
                },
                {
                  "id": "postorder_valid",
                  "text": "A one-visit postorder DFS can compute the sum."
                },
                {
                  "id": "bfs_valid",
                  "text": "A one-visit level-order BFS can compute the sum."
                },
                {
                  "id": "inorder_requires_bst",
                  "text": "Inorder can compute the sum only when the tree is a binary search tree."
                },
                {
                  "id": "bfs_only",
                  "text": "Only BFS is correct because addition must proceed by level."
                }
              ],
              "acceptedOptionIds": [
                "preorder_valid",
                "postorder_valid",
                "bfs_valid"
              ]
            },
            "scoringContract": {
              "type": "choice",
              "resultSemantics": "exact_selected_set_with_partial_v1"
            },
            "feedback": {
              "reason": "Addition over all nodes is order-independent, so any one-visit traversal is correct; the remaining choice concerns implementation and space, not the numeric result.",
              "details": "The sum is an associative, commutative aggregate over the set of node values. Preorder, inorder, postorder, and BFS all visit that set exactly once on a proper tree. A BST invariant is irrelevant to addition. The traversal still affects peak auxiliary state, streaming order, and opportunities for early stopping, so “order-independent result” does not mean every operational property is identical. The prompt's absence of level output or child-dependent logic is the decisive boundary.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "inorder_requires_bst": "Inorder needs a BST only to guarantee sorted output. It visits every node of any binary tree and can sum them correctly.",
                "bfs_only": "Addition does not require level grouping. BFS becomes preferable only when the output or stopping rule is depth-based."
              },
              "omittedCorrectExplanationsByOptionId": {
                "preorder_valid": "Preorder visits each actual node exactly once, and addition does not depend on action position.",
                "postorder_valid": "Postorder visits the same node set, and child-before-parent order does not alter the total.",
                "bfs_valid": "BFS also visits the same node set once, so it produces the same total despite different frontier storage."
              }
            },
            "taxonomy": {
