              "details": "Tree A has height `Θ(log n)` and maximum width `Θ(n)`, so recursive DFS uses `O(log n)` frames while the BFS queue can hold a linear-size level. Tree B has height `n` and width one, so recursive DFS uses `O(n)` frames while the queue holds at most one node and uses `O(1)` auxiliary space. Both traversals still take `O(n)` time when each node is processed once. These are shape-dependent bounds, not universal claims that DFS or BFS always uses less memory."
