            "feedback": {
              "reason": "A usable subproblem is defined by its inputs, its promised subtree result or effect, and its boundary meaning; implementation syntax follows from that contract.",
              "details": "The three questions make every recursive call interpretable in isolation. Inputs identify ancestor context or parameters. The promised result or effect tells the parent what it may rely on. The empty-subtree meaning closes the recursion and supplies an identity, sentinel, or failure value. Traversal order is then chosen from data dependencies and timing requirements. Starting with a global variable or a memorized traversal name often leaves hidden assumptions about reuse, null children, and branch combination.",
              "wrongOptionExplanationsByOptionId": {
